# Phase 5 — Centralized Entitlements Engine, Usage Metering & Server Gates

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Engineer a centralized, scatter-free entitlements and feature-gating engine (`src/lib/entitlements.ts`) that standardizes plan limits (maximum projects, member seats, API rate quotas, analytics retention) across `FREE`, `PRO`, and `TEAM` tiers. You will construct atomic server-side assertion guards executed inside Server Actions before database writes, build responsive usage progress meters in the UI, and create an Upgrade Dialog modal triggered when limits are reached.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Scatter-Free Entitlements Pattern
- **Anti-Pattern:** Inlining ad-hoc plan checks across 25 different components and Server Actions:
  ```typescript
  // Bad: Scattered business logic
  if (org.plan === 'FREE' && projects.length >= 3) { ... }
  ```
  If your pricing changes (e.g. Free gets 5 projects), you must hunt down dozens of files.
- **The Centralized Pattern:** Define a declarative `PLAN_LIMITS` configuration map. Both UI indicators (progress bars, upgrade badges) and server-side mutations import this single source of truth.

### 2. Cosmetic UI Gates vs Enforced Server Assertions
- **Client UI gates (Disabled buttons, locks):** Improve user experience by communicating limits before the user spends time typing.
- **Server Assertions (`assertCanCreateProject`):** True security boundary. An attacker can easily bypass a disabled React button by sending an HTTP POST directly to the Server Action. All mutations must assert limits atomically inside database transactions.

### 3. Graceful Plan Downgrade & Data Preservation
When a team downgrades from PRO (with 15 projects) to FREE (limit 3), their existing 15 projects **must never be deleted**. Instead, the entitlement engine transitions the workspace into an "over-quota" state: existing projects remain accessible and editable, but creating a 16th project is rejected until they upgrade or archive.

---

