# Phase 1 — Canvas 2D Engine, Hi-DPI Viewport Matrix & Scene Graph

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a buttery-smooth 60fps HTML5 Canvas 2D rendering engine in Next.js 15. You will solve device pixel ratio (`window.devicePixelRatio`) scaling so vector graphics render razor-sharp on Retina and 4K displays without pixelation. You will design an immutable scene graph data structure representing vector primitives (`rect`, `ellipse`, `line`, `text`), implement an affine 2D transform matrix (pan, infinite canvas navigation, pinch-to-zoom between 10% and 500%), and build a dirty-flag render loop driven by `requestAnimationFrame` that eliminates idle CPU consumption.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Canvas 2D Immediate Mode vs DOM/SVG Retained Mode
In collaborative graphics software (such as Figma or Miro), rendering performance is the primary UX constraint:
- **DOM / SVG Retained Mode:** Every circle, rectangle, or connector is an individual DOM element. When a canvas contains 1,500 shapes, dragging one shape triggers layout recalculations, style invalidations, and DOM reflow across the entire page, degrading frame rates to < 15 FPS.
- **Canvas 2D Immediate Mode:** The canvas is a single raw pixel buffer. Rendering is an explicit pure function `renderScene(ctx, shapes, viewport)`. Redrawing 10,000 shapes directly on the GPU canvas takes less than 3ms, maintaining a rock-solid 60 FPS.

### 2. The Retina / Hi-DPI Sub-Pixel Equation
Modern displays have pixel ratios of 2x or 3x (two or three physical device pixels per CSS layout pixel). If you set `<canvas width="800" height="600" />` on a Retina screen, the browser stretches the 800x600 bitmap across 1600x1200 physical pixels, causing blurry text and fuzzy edges.
- **The Fix:** Size the canvas backing buffer by `window.devicePixelRatio`, while maintaining CSS dimensions:
```typescript
canvas.width = Math.floor(cssWidth * dpr);
canvas.height = Math.floor(cssHeight * dpr);
canvas.style.width = `${cssWidth}px`;
canvas.style.height = `${cssHeight}px`;
ctx.scale(dpr, dpr);
```
All drawing coordinates can now be written in standard CSS pixels while rendering at native hardware sharpness.

### 3. Coordinate Space Duality: Screen Space vs World Space
An infinite canvas requires two distinct coordinate frames:
- **Screen Space:** Coordinates relative to the top-left corner of the browser canvas element (what the mouse pointer reports in `e.clientX - rect.left`).
- **World Space:** Conceptual infinite Cartesian whiteboard coordinates where shapes permanently live, unaffected by viewport pan or zoom.
- **Transformation Matrix Equations:**
  - Screen to World: `worldX = (screenX - panX) / zoom`
  - World to Screen: `screenX = (worldX * zoom) + panX`

---

