# Phase 2 — Pointer Interaction, Hit-Testing Math, Selection & Transform Handles

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement mathematical hit-testing algorithms across vector shapes (point-in-rectangle Axis-Aligned Bounding Box, point-in-ellipse algebraic equations, and line segment proximity distance). Construct an interactive pointer state machine that handles single selection, multi-selection bounding boxes, shape dragging with pointer capture (`setPointerCapture`), 8-point corner and edge transform handles with aspect-ratio preservation, and marquee rubber-band drag selection.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Inverse Transform Hit-Testing
When testing whether a mouse click at Screen coordinate `(150, 240)` hits a shape at World coordinate `(800, 1200)` with `zoom: 0.5` and `pan: (-200, -300)`:
- **Naive Approach:** Transform every shape in the scene into Screen Space and test against screen coordinates. For 2,000 shapes, this requires 2,000 matrix multiplications per frame.
- **Inverse Approach:** Transform the single mouse point from Screen Space into World Space once (`toWorld(pointer)`). Then evaluate geometric equations against raw shape coordinates. Hit testing completes in O(N) simple arithmetic operations.

### 2. Reverse Z-Index Raycasting
Shapes are stored in array order `[0, 1, 2, ..., N-1]`, where index `N-1` is visually on top.
- Hit testing iterates in reverse order from `N-1` down to `0`.
- The first shape that returns `true` is the topmost shape under the cursor. We immediately return it and break early.

### 3. Pointer Capture & Extreme Velocity Dragging
When a user drags a shape rapidly toward the screen margin, the operating system pointer can leave the browser canvas bounds between frame events.
- Calling `canvas.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId)` locks all operating system pointer events to the canvas until released, guaranteeing continuous drag tracking and zero dropped shapes.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Mathematical Hit-Testing Algorithms
Create `src/lib/hit-test.ts`:

```typescript
// src/lib/hit-test.ts
import { CanvasShape, Point } from "@/types/canvas";

/**
 * Tests if a point in World Space hits a given shape
 */
export function hitTestShape(point: Point, shape: CanvasShape): boolean {
  switch (shape.type) {
    case "rect": {
      return (
        point.x >= shape.x &&
        point.x <= shape.x + shape.w &&
        point.y >= shape.y &&
        point.y <= shape.y + shape.h
      );
    }

    case "ellipse": {
      const rx = Math.abs(shape.w) / 2;
      const ry = Math.abs(shape.h) / 2;
      const cx = shape.x + rx;
      const cy = shape.y + ry;
      if (rx === 0 || ry === 0) return false;
      // Normalized ellipse equation: ((x-cx)^2 / rx^2) + ((y-cy)^2 / ry^2) <= 1
      const normalizedDist = Math.pow(point.x - cx, 2) / Math.pow(rx, 2) + Math.pow(point.y - cy, 2) / Math.pow(ry, 2);
      return normalizedDist <= 1.0;
    }

    case "line": {
      // Distance from point to line segment
      const dist = distanceToSegment(point, { x: shape.x, y: shape.y }, { x: shape.x2, y: shape.y2 });
      return dist <= Math.max(shape.strokeWidth / 2, 6);
    }

    default:
      return false;
  }
}

function distanceToSegment(p: Point, v: Point, w: Point): number {
  const l2 = Math.pow(v.x - w.x, 2) + Math.pow(v.y - w.y, 2);
  if (l2 === 0) return Math.hypot(p.x - v.x, p.y - v.y);
  let t = ((p.x - v.x) * (w.x - v.x) + (p.y - v.y) * (w.y - v.y)) / l2;
  t = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t));
  return Math.hypot(p.x - (v.x + t * (w.x - v.x)), p.y - (v.y + t * (w.y - v.y)));
}

/**
 * Raycasts through all shapes in reverse Z-order to return the topmost clicked shape
 */
export function findTopShapeAtPoint(point: Point, shapes: CanvasShape[]): CanvasShape | null {
  for (let i = shapes.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (hitTestShape(point, shapes[i])) {
      return shapes[i];
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

### Step 2 · Transform Handles & Bounding Box Calculations
Create `src/lib/transform-handles.ts`. This module defines the 8 resize handle positions for selected shapes:

```typescript
// src/lib/transform-handles.ts
import { CanvasShape, Point, ViewportTransform } from "@/types/canvas";
import { worldToScreen } from "@/lib/matrix";

export type HandleType = "nw" | "n" | "ne" | "e" | "se" | "s" | "sw" | "w";

export interface TransformHandle {
  type: HandleType;
  screenX: number;
  screenY: number;
  worldX: number;
  worldY: number;
}

export function getShapeHandles(shape: CanvasShape, viewport: ViewportTransform): TransformHandle[] {
  const { x, y, w, h } = shape;
  const midX = x + w / 2;
  const midY = y + h / 2;

  const rawPositions: { type: HandleType; worldX: number; worldY: number }[] = [
    { type: "nw", worldX: x, worldY: y },
    { type: "n", worldX: midX, worldY: y },
    { type: "ne", worldX: x + w, worldY: y },
    { type: "e", worldX: x + w, worldY: midY },
    { type: "se", worldX: x + w, worldY: y + h },
    { type: "s", worldX: midX, worldY: y + h },
    { type: "sw", worldX: x, worldY: y + h },
    { type: "w", worldX: x, worldY: midY },
  ];

  return rawPositions.map((p) => {
    const screen = worldToScreen({ x: p.worldX, y: p.worldY }, viewport);
    return {
      type: p.type,
      screenX: screen.x,
      screenY: screen.y,
      worldX: p.worldX,
      worldY: p.worldY,
    };
  });
}

export function hitTestHandle(screenPoint: Point, handles: TransformHandle[]): HandleType | null {
  const handleRadius = 7;
  for (const h of handles) {
    if (Math.hypot(screenPoint.x - h.screenX, screenPoint.y - h.screenY) <= handleRadius) {
      return h.type;
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

