# Phase 1 — WebSocket Server Architecture, Strict Envelope Protocol & Connection Multiplexing

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a production-grade, standalone Node.js and TypeScript WebSocket server using the high-performance `ws` library. Define an enterprise discriminated-union wire protocol using Zod to strictly validate every inbound and outbound frame, enforce maximum frame sizes to prevent memory exhaustion, implement heartbeat ping/pong detection to prune dead TCP half-open connections, and maintain a centralized socket connection registry.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The WebSocket Handshake (RFC 6455)

A WebSocket connection begins as an HTTP/1.1 request containing specific upgrade headers. The client initiates with `Upgrade: websocket` and `Connection: Upgrade`, along with a base64-encoded `Sec-WebSocket-Key`. The server verifies the key, computes a SHA-1 hash using the RFC 6455 magic GUID (`258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11`), and responds with HTTP status `101 Switching Protocols`.

```
Client                                          Server
  |                                                |
  | --- HTTP/1.1 GET /ws (Upgrade: websocket) ---> |
  |     Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==|
  |                                                |
  | <-- HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols --------- | (SHA-1 hash handshake)
  |     Sec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pPLMBiTxaQ9kYGzhZlbgf7gUk=
  |                                                |
  | <====== Bidirectional Full-Duplex Framing =====> |
  | [Text Frame: Opcode 0x1, 4-byte Masked Payload] |
  |                                                |
  | --- Ping Frame (Opcode 0x9) -----------------> |
  | <-- Pong Frame (Opcode 0xA) ------------------ |
```

### Frame Masking & Framing Overhead

Under RFC 6455, all frames sent from client to server MUST be masked with a random 4-byte masking key to prevent cache poisoning attacks in transparent HTTP proxies. Server-to-client frames are unmasked. Framing adds only 2 to 14 bytes of overhead per message, contrasting dramatically with HTTP request headers (often 500B–2KB per request).

### Solving TCP Half-Open Zombie Connections

When a mobile client moves into a tunnel or drops Wi-Fi abruptly, no TCP `FIN` or `RST` packet is sent to the server. The operating system kernel on the server retains the socket in the `ESTABLISHED` state indefinitely until keepalive probes fail (often 2 hours by default). If left unmonitored, thousands of zombie sockets exhaust server file descriptors (`ulimit -n`). We implement an application-level ping/pong heartbeat timer that sweeps active connections every 30 seconds and calls `socket.terminate()` on silent sockets.

---

