- [ ] Attempting to connect without a token returns `HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized`.
- [ ] Connecting from an unapproved Origin (e.g. `http://malicious.org`) returns `HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden`.
- [ ] Providing a valid signed JWT passes the handshake and attaches verified claims to the socket.
- [ ] Flooding more than 10 messages per second triggers a `RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` error frame.
- [ ] Sockets cleanly unregister from the connection registry upon closing without leaving zombie references.

# Phase 3 — Rooms & Redis Pub/Sub Distributed Message Fan-Out

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Scale the chat infrastructure horizontally across multiple Node.js instances behind a load balancer using Redis Pub/Sub. Implement channel-based room multiplexing, prevent message self-echoing, dynamic Redis subscription management, and resilience against Redis disconnects with buffer queues.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Horizontal Scaling Challenge

When multiple Node.js servers run behind an AWS ALB or NGINX reverse proxy, User A's WebSocket terminates on Server 1, while User B's WebSocket terminates on Server 2. If User A posts a message to `#general`, Server 1 has no direct memory access to Server 2's TCP sockets. We solve this by introducing Redis Pub/Sub as the shared message bus.

```
   [ Browser A ]                [ Browser B ]                [ Browser C ]
         |                            |                            |
(Instance 1 Sockets)         (Instance 2 Sockets)         (Instance 2 Sockets)
         |                            |                            |
    [ Node 1 ]                   [ Node 2 ]                   [ Node 2 ]
         \                            /                            /
          \                          /                            /
       PUBLISH                    SUBSCRIBE                    SUBSCRIBE
    channel: room:123          channel: room:123            channel: room:123
            \                      /                            /
             +--------------------+----------------------------+
                                  |
                         [ Redis Pub/Sub Bus ]
```

### Why Two Separate Redis Client Connections Are Mandatory

In Redis, when a client executes `SUBSCRIBE` or `PSUBSCRIBE`, that client connection enters subscriber mode. The Redis server will ONLY accept channel subscription commands (`SUBSCRIBE`, `UNSUBSCRIBE`, `PING`, `QUIT`) on that connection. Any attempt to execute `PUBLISH`, `GET`, or `SET` on a subscribed client connection throws a fatal Redis error. Therefore, every Node.js instance must maintain at least **two** independent Redis client connections: one dedicated exclusively to Subscriptions, and one for Publishing and key/value operations.

### Eliminating Self-Echo Loops

When a client sends a message, Instance 1 publishes the message to Redis. Because Instance 1 is also subscribed to `room:{id}`, it receives its own published message back from Redis. If Instance 1 blindly broadcasts it to all local room members, the original sender receives an annoying duplicate frame. We attach an ephemeral `originInstanceId` and `senderSocketId` to every Redis wire payload to filter out the originating socket.

---

