# Phase 1 — Domain Model, Prisma Schema & Order Finite State Machine

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a complete, enterprise-grade e-commerce domain model using PostgreSQL and Prisma ORM. Implement `Product`, `Variant` with atomic inventory fields (`stock`, `reserved`), `Order`, `OrderLine` with frozen price snapshots, and an immutable Order Finite State Machine (FSM) enforcing transition invariants (`PENDING -> PAID -> FULFILLED | CANCELLED`).

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### E-Commerce Domain Invariants: Stock vs. Reserved

In high-velocity commerce, inventory cannot simply be a single `quantity` integer. When a user clicks "Checkout", their items must be held while they complete payment. If you decrement `stock` immediately, an abandoned checkout permanently loses goods from your catalog. If you don't hold items, another shopper can purchase the exact same item, resulting in overselling.

We separate inventory into two distinct columns on every product variant:
- `stock`: Physical units physically present in the warehouse.
- `reserved`: Units locked in active, unexpired checkouts.
- **Available Inventory** = `stock - reserved`.

### The Historical Price Drift Bug

Never link historical order line items directly to the `Variant` table for price lookups. If a merchant edits a T-shirt price from $25 to $40 next week, querying past orders would retroactively show that customers paid $40, corrupting past tax filings, accounting records, and invoices. Every `OrderLine` must freeze and persist a static snapshot of the SKU, product title, and `unitPriceCents` at the exact moment the order is created.

### Finite State Machine (FSM) for Order Lifecycles

```
                    [ PENDING ] (Checkout Initiated / Stock Reserved)
                     /       \
     (Payment Fails /         \ (Stripe Webhook Confirms Payment)
      Cart Abandons)           v
           v                [ PAID ] (Ready for Warehouse Dispatch)
     [ CANCELLED ]             /
     (Stock Released)         / (BullMQ Fulfillment Finishes)
                             v
                       [ FULFILLED ]
```

Enforcing valid state transitions within a centralized FSM engine prevents invalid operations—such as fulfilling an order that was never paid, or refunding a cancelled order.

