# Phase 3 — Idempotent Checkout Pipeline & Stripe PaymentIntents

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a production-grade, crash-resilient checkout endpoint integrating Stripe PaymentIntents. Enforce strict HTTP `Idempotency-Key` headers, persist checkout intents with unique database constraints to prevent duplicate charges upon network retries, and securely verify Stripe signature webhooks.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Idempotency Key Pattern (IETF Standard)

In mobile and web e-commerce, networks are inherently unreliable. When a customer taps "Place Order", the request may reach the server, process the payment, and create the order—but the cellular connection drops before the HTTP 200 response reaches the client. The browser or mobile app automatically retries the request.

Without idempotency protection, the customer is billed twice and two identical orders are created. We implement the standard `Idempotency-Key` header:

```
Client                                          Server & Stripe
  |                                                    |
  | --- POST /api/checkout --------------------------> |
  |     Idempotency-Key: 7b3e6d8a-9281-4231            |
  |                                                    | [1] Check DB: Key exists?
  |                                                    |     NO -> Create Order & Stripe Intent
  |                                                    |     Store Key in DB
  | <--- (Network packet dropped!) ------------------- | 
  |
  | --- RETRY: POST /api/checkout -------------------> |
  |     Idempotency-Key: 7b3e6d8a-9281-4231            |
  |                                                    | [2] Check DB: Key exists?
  |                                                    |     YES -> Return cached original response!
  | <--- HTTP 200 OK (Original Order + ClientSecret) - | (No new charge created)
```

### Why Stripe Webhooks Are Mandatory

Never mark an order as `PAID` based on a frontend redirect or browser callback. If a buyer closes their browser tab the moment 3D Secure completes, the redirect never fires. Furthermore, malicious users can spoof client-side redirect URLs. The **only** authoritative source of truth for payment success is Stripe's server-to-server webhook (`payment_intent.succeeded`).

