# Phase 4 — Background Fulfillment Engine with BullMQ & Distributed Retries

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a reliable, asynchronous background job fulfillment pipeline using BullMQ and Redis. Offload heavy post-payment workflows (inventory finalization, digital license generation, PDF invoice creation, email receipts, shipping label generation) to worker processes with exponential backoff, rate limiting, and a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ).

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Synchronous Webhooks vs Asynchronous Worker Queues

When Stripe sends a `payment_intent.succeeded` webhook, it expects an HTTP 200 response within **10 seconds**. If your server takes 12 seconds generating PDF invoices and contacting shipping carrier APIs, Stripe marks the webhook as timed out and continuously resends it, triggering duplicate fulfillment runs.

**The Solution**: The webhook handler performs only two operations: mark the order as `PAID` in PostgreSQL, and push a job `{ orderId }` into a BullMQ Redis queue. The HTTP 200 response returns in **under 30ms**, while background worker processes consume and fulfill jobs safely.

```
[ Stripe Webhook ]
       |
       v (HTTP 200 in 25ms)
[ Express Server ] ---> [ BullMQ Queue: "order-fulfillment" ]
                                    |
                   +----------------+----------------+
                   |                                 |
             [ Worker 1 ]                      [ Worker 2 ]
                   |
        1. Finalize Stock (Deduct physical stock)
        2. Generate PDF Invoice & Email Receipt
        3. Mark Order FULFILLED in DB
        (On 5 failures -> Move to Dead Letter Queue)
```

### Idempotent Worker Processing

Workers must be designed under the assumption that a job **will** be executed more than once (e.g., worker container crashes mid-execution, or Redis lease expires). If a worker decrements inventory without checking order state, running the job twice will decrement stock twice. Every worker must check:  
`if (order.status !== 'PAID') return;`  
Only execute transitions if the order is in the expected state.

---

