# Phase 5 — Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Admin Engine & React Dashboard

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a secure multi-role administrative system (SUPER_ADMIN, INVENTORY_MANAGER, SUPPORT_AGENT) using Express authorization middleware and a React TypeScript admin dashboard. Implement granular permission matrices, audit logs for catalog mutations, paginated product/order tables with full-text search, and stock adjustment modals.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Authentication vs. Authorization in Admin Systems

- **Authentication (AuthN)**: Proves identity via credentials or JWT tokens ("This user is John Doe").
- **Authorization (AuthZ)**: Governs capabilities based on roles or permission policies ("Does John Doe have permission to modify warehouse inventory or issue refunds?").

### The Security Boundary Is Exclusively the API

In frontend SPAs, hiding a "Delete Product" button or disabling a form field is an ergonomics convenience—**not a security boundary**. Any user with Chrome DevTools can construct a `DELETE /api/admin/products/:id` HTTP request. Every administrative route on the Express server must validate the incoming JWT claims against an explicit RBAC permission matrix.

```
Role Permissions Matrix:
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Permission              | CUSTOMER | SUPPORT | INVENTORY_MGR | ADMIN
----------------------------------------------------------------------
VIEW_CATALOG            |   YES    |   YES   |     YES       |  YES
PLACE_ORDER             |   YES    |   NO    |     NO        |  YES
VIEW_ORDERS             |   OWN    |   ALL   |     ALL       |  ALL
UPDATE_INVENTORY        |   NO     |   NO    |     YES       |  YES
EDIT_PRODUCT_PRICING    |   NO     |   NO    |     NO        |  YES
ISSUE_REFUND            |   NO     |   NO    |     NO        |  YES
VIEW_AUDIT_LOGS         |   NO     |   NO    |     NO        |  YES
```

### Immutable Audit Logging for Stock Adjustments

Whenever a manager adjusts warehouse stock (e.g. damaged goods or manual cycle count), the system must log an immutable audit record containing: `userId`, `sku`, `previousStock`, `newStock`, `delta`, `reason`, and `timestamp`. This prevents employee shrinkage and creates accountability.

