# Phase 3 — Business Service Layer, Immutable DTOs & RFC 7807 Problem Details

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a clean service layer with transactional boundaries (`@Transactional`), immutable Java 21 `record` DTOs, Bean Validation constraints (`@NotBlank`, `@Size`, `@FutureOrPresent`), and centralized exception handling via `@RestControllerAdvice` conforming to the RFC 7807 Problem Details for HTTP APIs standard.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Pure DTO Boundary (Never Expose Entities)

Returning JPA entities directly from Spring `@RestController` methods introduces three major vulnerabilities:
1. **Mass Assignment Vulnerability**: Attackers send `{ "id": 99, "owner": { "id": 1 } }` in request bodies, hijacking ownership.
2. **LazyInitializationException**: Jackson attempts to serialize uninitialized lazy proxies outside the transaction, crashing with HTTP 500.
3. **Information Disclosure**: Internal fields like `passwordHash` or soft-delete flags leak to public clients.

We enforce Java 21 Records as strictly immutable Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) for all HTTP ingress and egress.

### Standardizing RFC 7807 Problem Details

Rather than returning ad-hoc error structures, Spring Boot 3 natively supports **RFC 7807** (`application/problem+json`). Every error returned by our API adheres to a standardized schema:

```json
{
  "type": "https://buildscape.io/errors/validation-failed",
  "title": "Validation Failed",
  "status": 400,
  "detail": "One or more request parameters failed validation.",
  "instance": "/api/v1/tasks",
  "invalidParams": [
    { "field": "title", "message": "Title must not be blank" }
  ],
  "timestamp": "2026-10-03T02:10:00Z"
}
```

### Declarative `@Transactional` Semantics

All mutation methods in the service layer are annotated with `@Transactional`. If any runtime exception occurs (e.g., `ResourceNotFoundException`), Spring AOP automatically issues a database `ROLLBACK`. Read-only methods are marked `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` to instruct Hibernate to disable dirty-checking overhead, boosting read throughput.

