# Phase 4 — Spring Security 6 with Stateless JWT Authentication & BCrypt

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement modern stateless authentication using Spring Security 6, `SecurityFilterChain` bean DSL, BCrypt password hashing, and signed JSON Web Tokens (HMAC-SHA256). Create a `OncePerRequestFilter` to intercept requests, validate JWT signatures, and establish the `SecurityContextHolder` without HTTP sessions.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Spring Security 6 Modern Architecture

Spring Security 6 completely removed `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`. Instead, security is configured declaratively using `@Bean` definitions of `SecurityFilterChain`. The filter chain operates as a pipeline of servlet filters:

```
Incoming HTTP Request
       |
       v
[ CorsFilter ] ------------------------> Verifies Allowed Frontend Origins
       |
       v
[ JwtAuthenticationFilter ] -----------> Extracts 'Authorization: Bearer <JWT>'
       |                                 Verifies HMAC-SHA256 Signature
       |                                 Sets Authentication in SecurityContextHolder
       v
[ UsernamePasswordAuthFilter ] --------> Bypassed (Stateless REST API)
       |
       v
[ FilterSecurityInterceptor ] ---------> Checks .requestMatchers("/api/v1/auth/**").permitAll()
       |                                 Checks .anyRequest().authenticated()
       v
[ DispatcherServlet -> Controller ]
```

### Stateless Session Creation Policy

In standard web applications, Spring Security creates an HTTP session (`JSESSIONID` cookie) on the server. In a distributed cloud environment with multiple backend instances, sticky sessions or Redis session replication are required. By configuring `SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS`, Spring Security **never** creates an HTTP session. Every single request is authenticated independently via its Bearer token.

### Why CSRF Is Disabled in Stateless REST APIs

Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) relies on the browser automatically attaching ambient credentials (cookies) to cross-origin requests. Because our API uses Bearer tokens stored in client memory or headers (which browsers NEVER send automatically across origins), CSRF attacks are fundamentally impossible. Therefore, `http.csrf(AbstractHttpConfigurer::disable)` is both safe and standard for stateless JWT architectures.

