# Phase 5 — Nightly Automated Reconciliation Engine & Forensic Drift Auditing

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build an enterprise financial reconciliation engine in Spring Boot 3.3. Implement high-speed relational audit queries that continuously verify the core ledger invariant: every account's cached balance (`balance_cache`) must strictly equal the aggregate sum of all its immutable postings ($\sum \text{entry}.\text{amount}$). Build point-in-time forensic balance reconstruction for regulatory compliance, persist audit run outcomes in dedicated compliance tables (`reconciliation_run`), and construct automated incident alerting for detected drift.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Separation of Derived State and Truth
In banking ledgers, performance constraints require maintaining a `balance_cache` column on the `account` table for sub-millisecond authorization during transfers. However, in financial engineering:
$$\text{Truth} \equiv \text{Immutable Entry Log} \quad \neq \quad \text{Cached Balance}$$

If a database crash, human DBA tampering, or application edge case ever desynchronizes the cache from the entries, the bank is in regulatory violation.  
**Automated Reconciliation** acts as an impartial auditor running continuously:  
For every account $A$:
$$\Delta_A = \text{balance\_cache}_A - \sum_{e \in \text{entries}(A)} e.\text{amount}$$
Invariant: $\Delta_A = 0$. If $\Delta_A \neq 0$, an immutable audit discrepancy is logged and a severity-1 alert is triggered.

```
Reconciliation Audit Pipeline:
[ Scheduled Cron (2:00 AM UTC) or Admin Trigger ]
                       |
                       v
[ High-Speed Aggregation Query (GROUP BY account.id HAVING diff <> 0) ]
                       |
        +--------------+--------------+
        |                             |
   (Zero Rows)                 (Discrepancies Found)
        v                             v
[ Status: CLEAN ]             [ Log Discrepancies to DB ]
[ Record Audit Run ]          [ Dispatch PagerDuty / Webhook Alert ]
                              [ Lock Compromised Account for Audit ]
```

### 2. High-Performance SQL Aggregation with HAVING
Querying accounts one-by-one requires millions of database round-trips. We utilize a single PostgreSQL aggregation query using `LEFT JOIN` and `HAVING`:
```sql
SELECT a.id, a.balance_cache, COALESCE(SUM(e.amount), 0) AS calculated_sum,
       (a.balance_cache - COALESCE(SUM(e.amount), 0)) AS drift
FROM account a
LEFT JOIN entry e ON e.account_id = a.id
GROUP BY a.id, a.balance_cache
HAVING a.balance_cache <> COALESCE(SUM(e.amount), 0);
```
On a healthy database, this query executes in single-digit seconds across millions of rows and returns 0 rows. Any returning row indicates a balance corruption.

### 3. Historical Point-in-Time Balance Reconstruction
Regulators frequently demand historical ledger audits (e.g. *"Prove Account X had exactly $4,120.50 USD on 2025-12-31 at 23:59:59 UTC"*). Because entry records are strictly append-only and timestamped, we reconstruct the exact balance at any point in history $T$:
$$\text{Balance}(A, T) = \sum_{e \in \text{entries}(A) \land e.\text{created\_at} \le T} e.\text{amount}$$

