# Phase 2 — Resilient HTTP Transport, Timeouts & Memory-Bounded Fetching

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Engineer an enterprise-grade HTTP fetching engine on top of Go's `net/http` package capable of crawling diverse web servers safely and reliably. Completely avoid the silent fatal traps of Go's `http.DefaultClient` (which has no timeouts and leaks sockets indefinitely). Configure a customized `http.Transport` tuned for high-throughput concurrent crawling with aggressive idle connection pooling (`MaxIdleConns`, `MaxIdleConnsPerHost`, `IdleConnTimeout`), dual-stack TCP keep-alives, and TLS handshake timeouts. Enforce hard per-request deadlines using `context.WithTimeout`. Protect your crawler against memory exhaustion (OOM) attacks from malicious or oversized media files by streaming response bodies through `io.LimitReader` capped at 2 megabytes. Author a custom redirect policy to prevent open-redirect escape, implement exponential backoff retries for transient 5xx server errors, and construct a comprehensive test suite using Go's standard `httptest.Server`.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Perils of Go's `http.DefaultClient` & Socket Leaks
Many beginners write `resp, err := http.Get(url)`. In a web crawler, this is a catastrophic anti-pattern:
- `http.DefaultClient` and `http.DefaultTransport` specify **zero timeout** (`Timeout: 0`). If a target server accepts a TCP connection but halts transmission mid-stream ("slowloris" behavior), the goroutine blocks forever.
- Each stalled connection holds open a file descriptor and kernel socket buffers. Under high concurrency, the OS rapidly exhausts available file descriptors (`socket: too many open files`), crashing the crawler process.

### 2. TCP Connection Pooling & Keep-Alive Mechanics
Creating a fresh TCP connection for every single HTTP request is computationally expensive:
- TCP 3-way handshake: 1 RTT (Round Trip Time)
- TLS 1.3 handshake: 1 to 2 RTTs
- Slow-start TCP congestion window ramp-up

By tuning `http.Transport`, we reuse existing open TCP connections across consecutive requests to the same origin host:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                            HTTP TRANSPORT CONNECTION POOL                               |
|                                                                                         |
|  Worker Goroutines                http.Transport Connection Pool           Target Host  |
|                                                                                         |
|  [ Worker 1 ] -------
|                      \            +----------------------------+        https://target.com
|  [ Worker 2 ] --------+---------> | Reusable Idle TCP Sockets  | ====> [ Established ]  |
|                      /            | MaxIdleConnsPerHost: 10    |       [ Keep-Alive  ]  |
|  [ Worker 3 ] -------             +----------------------------+                        |
|                                                                                         |
|  Benefits:                                                                              |
|  - Zero TCP/TLS handshake overhead on repeated requests to the same domain              |
|  - Idle connections pruned automatically after IdleConnTimeout (90s)                    |
|  - DialContext enforces strict 10s connection establishment ceiling                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 3. Memory Protection: Streaming Limits vs `io.ReadAll` OOM Hazard
A malicious or misconfigured web page might serve a 5-gigabyte disk image or ISO file under `Content-Type: text/html`. Calling `io.ReadAll(resp.Body)` buffers the entire stream into memory until the operating system invokes the Out-Of-Memory (OOM) killer to terminate your process.

By wrapping `resp.Body` with `io.LimitReader(resp.Body, 2 * 1024 * 1024)`, we guarantee that no single HTTP response can consume more than 2MB of RAM, truncating oversized files while safely logging a warning.

