# Phase 3 — HTML Tokenization, Canonical URL Normalization & Link Extraction

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build an industrial-grade HTML parsing and URL canonicalization pipeline in Go. Web crawlers face a chaotic web where links exist in countless non-standard, relative, malformed, or redundant states: root-relative (`/docs`), relative to directory (`sub/page`), parent relative (`../about`), fragment-appended (`#section2`), tracking-parameter-laden (`?utm_source=twitter&session=xyz`), or pointing to non-HTTP protocols (`mailto:`, `javascript:`, `tel:`). You will implement a high-throughput, low-memory streaming HTML tokenizer using `golang.org/x/net/html` that extracts `<a href>` hyperlinks without building expensive DOM trees. You will build an RFC 3986 canonical URL normalizer that cleanses tracking parameters, removes fragments, strips redundant trailing slashes, and resolves relative references. Finally, you will construct a domain boundary filter and an HTML metadata extractor for page titles and descriptions, thoroughly verified with extensive unit and benchmark test suites.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Streaming Tokenizer vs DOM Tree Parsing Memory Footprint
Many HTML libraries (and Go's standard `html.Parse(reader)`) construct an in-memory Abstract Syntax Tree (DOM) where every HTML tag, attribute, and text node is instantiated as a heap-allocated `*html.Node` struct linked by pointers (`Parent`, `FirstChild`, `LastChild`, `PrevSibling`, `NextSibling`). 

For a large web page (e.g., a 1MB HTML document with 30,000 DOM elements):
- A full DOM parser allocates hundreds of thousands of small heap objects, causing massive GC pressure and latency spikes across concurrent worker threads.
- In contrast, Go's `html.NewTokenizer(reader)` is a zero-allocation streaming scanner. It moves a sliding window buffer across raw bytes, returning tokens (`StartTagToken`, `EndTagToken`, `SelfClosingTagToken`, `TextToken`) sequentially. You inspect tag names and attributes directly from byte slices without materializing unnecessary node trees.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                    STREAMING TOKENIZER VS FULL DOM TREE MEMORY                          |
|                                                                                         |
|  Approach A: DOM Tree Parser (html.Parse)                                               |
|  [ Raw HTML ] ===> [ Node Allocator ] ===> [ Complex 100,000+ Node Pointer Graph ]     |
|                                            * Heap footprint: 15MB - 40MB per page       |
|                                            * High Garbage Collector pause time          |
|                                                                                         |
|  Approach B: Streaming Tokenizer (html.NewTokenizer)  <-- OUR ARCHITECTURE              |
|  [ Raw HTML ] ===> [ 4KB Tokenizer Buffer ] ===> Stream Tokens (StartTag, Text, etc.)   |
|                                            * Heap footprint: O(1) buffer reuse          |
|                                            * Zero DOM graph allocations                 |
|                                            * Discards non-link tags instantly           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Canonical URL Normalization Pipeline
Duplicate URLs are the single greatest threat to crawler efficiency. If two different string representations point to the exact same web resource, crawling both wastes network bandwidth, duplicates database entries, and creates potential infinite crawl loops. Canonical normalization transforms any arbitrary URL into a unique, standardized identifier.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         CANONICAL URL NORMALIZATION PIPELINE                            |
|                                                                                         |
|   Raw href: "  /blog/Go-Concurrency/index.html?utm_source=news#section-1  "           |
|                                     |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   1. String Trim & Validation       "/blog/Go-Concurrency/index.html?..."               |
|                                     |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   2. Base URL Resolution            "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency/..."       |
|      (RFC 3986 ResolveReference)    |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   3. Lowercase Scheme & Host        "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency/..."       |
|      (Path casing preserved!)       |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   4. Strip Fragment Anchor          "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency/index.html|
|      (Drop "#section-1")            ?utm_source=news"                                   |
|                                     |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   5. Query Param Scrubbing          "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency/index.html|
|      (Remove utm_*, fbclid, ref)    |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   6. Directory Index Stripping      "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency"          |
|      (Remove /index.html & trail /) |                                                   |
|                                     v                                                   |
|   CANONICAL URL OUTPUT:             "https://example.com/blog/Go-Concurrency"          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 3. Protocol and Extension Boundary Filtering
Web pages contain links with exotic URL schemes (`mailto:contact@site.com`, `tel:+123456789`, `javascript:void(0);`, `data:image/png;base64,...`) and references to heavy binary assets (`.pdf`, `.zip`, `.dmg`, `.tar.gz`, `.mp4`). If the crawler feeds these into HTTP workers:
- Non-HTTP schemes cause request builder errors.
- Binary files waste bandwidth and cannot be parsed for HTML links.

Filtering must happen immediately at link discovery before URLs enter the crawler's work queue.

