# Phase 4 — Thread-Safe Visited Registry, Metrics & Token-Bucket Rate Limiting

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Construct the thread-safe state synchronization and polite network rate-limiting infrastructure for the crawler. Concurrently running worker goroutines must query and update a shared deduplication registry millions of times without data races or memory corruption. You will implement a high-performance concurrent `VisitedSet` backed by `sync.RWMutex` featuring atomic test-and-set semantics (`CheckAndAdd(url string) bool`). You will construct lock-free telemetry counters using Go's `sync/atomic` to record total crawled pages, byte throughput, HTTP error frequencies, and discovered link totals without mutex contention. You will integrate a token-bucket rate limiter with `golang.org/x/time/rate` enforcing strict requests-per-second constraints and configurable burst capacities. You will build a polite `robots.txt` compliance parser and evaluate the entire subsystem under intense concurrency stress tests with 500 concurrent goroutines, validated with the Go race detector (`go test -race`).

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Fatal Hazard of Concurrent Go Map Access & `sync.RWMutex` Invariants
In Go, built-in `map` types are **not** safe for concurrent use. If two or more goroutines access a map concurrently and at least one writes, the Go runtime crashes the entire process immediately with `fatal error: concurrent map read and map write`. Unlike standard exceptions, this runtime panic cannot be recovered with `recover()`—it terminates the process unconditionally.

To ensure thread safety, we wrap the map in a struct guarded by `sync.RWMutex`:
- Multiple worker goroutines holding read locks (`RLock()`) can inspect the set simultaneously with zero serialization bottleneck.
- When a worker needs to insert a newly discovered URL, it acquires an exclusive write lock (`Lock()`), guaranteeing isolated memory mutation.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        TOCTOU RACE VS ATOMIC CHECK-AND-ADD                              |
|                                                                                         |
|  Flawed Approach: Non-Atomic TOCTOU (Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use)                      |
|  Goroutine 1: if !seen.Contains("/page") {                                              |
|  Goroutine 2: if !seen.Contains("/page") {  <-- Both check simultaneously: true!        |
|  Goroutine 1:     seen.Add("/page")                                                     |
|  Goroutine 2:     seen.Add("/page")          <-- Duplicated fetch executed twice!       |
|                                                                                         |
|  Robust Architecture: Atomic CheckAndAdd Under Exclusive Write Lock                     |
|  Goroutine 1: seen.mu.Lock()                                                            |
|               if _, exists := seen.m["/page"]; !exists {                                |
|                   seen.m["/page"] = struct{}{}                                          |
|                   seen.mu.Unlock() -> returns true (Worker 1 crawls URL)                |
|               }                                                                         |
|  Goroutine 2: seen.mu.Lock() (waited for Goroutine 1)                                   |
|               if _, exists := seen.m["/page"]; exists {                                 |
|                   seen.mu.Unlock() -> returns false (Worker 2 skips duplicate!)         |
|               }                                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Token-Bucket Rate Limiting Algorithm (`golang.org/x/time/rate`)
Web scraping without rate limits behaves like an accidental Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack against web hosts, prompting immediate IP bans or HTTP `429 Too Many Requests` errors.

The Token Bucket algorithm regulates request timing:
- The bucket has a maximum capacity $b$ (burst limit) and continuously refills with tokens at rate $r$ tokens/second.
- Every worker wanting to dispatch an HTTP request must acquire 1 token from the bucket.
- If tokens are available, the worker proceeds immediately without delay.
- If the bucket is empty, the worker blocks cooperatively until a token replenishes or the context cancels.

$$\text{Tokens}(t) = \min\big(b, \; \text{Tokens}(t_{\text{prev}}) + (t - t_{\text{prev}}) \times r\big)$$

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         TOKEN-BUCKET RATE LIMITER PIPELINE                              |
|                                                                                         |
|       Refill Generator (e.g. 10 tokens / second)                                        |
|                          |                                                              |
|                          v                                                              |
|             +--------------------------+                                                |
|             |  Token Bucket (Cap: 10)  |  (Refills smoothly over time)                  |
|             |  [o] [o] [o] [o] [o]     |                                                |
|             +--------------------------+                                                |
|                          |                                                              |
|                          | Consumes 1 token per request                                 |
|                          v                                                              |
|    Worker 1 ----> [ limiter.Wait(ctx) ] ===> Proceeds immediately                       |
|    Worker 2 ----> [ limiter.Wait(ctx) ] ===> Proceeds immediately                       |
|    Worker 3 ----> [ limiter.Wait(ctx) ] ===> Pauses 100ms until next token generates    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 3. Lock-Free Metrics with `sync/atomic`
In high-throughput crawlers processing hundreds of pages per second, using a mutex to increment simple counters (pages crawled, bytes downloaded, errors encountered) creates severe CPU cache-line bouncing and lock contention across dozens of worker goroutines.

Go 1.19+ `sync/atomic` types (`atomic.Uint64`, `atomic.Int64`) provide hardware-level atomic instructions (`LOCK XADD` on x86-64), enabling single-nanosecond telemetry recording without mutex serialization.

