# Phase 5 — Concurrent Worker Pool, Channel Pipelines & Deadlock-Free Coordination

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Architect and construct a production-ready concurrent worker pool and dynamic coordination engine in Go. A web crawler is not a static linear pipeline: newly crawled pages emit child URLs that must feed back into the work queue, creating a recursive circular dependency. If workers attempt to push child URLs directly into a bounded input channel they are also draining, the entire system deadlocks as soon as channel buffers fill. You will implement a deadlock-free central coordinator that decouples job feeding from result collection using an in-memory frontier queue and an atomic in-flight task tracker. You will build worker goroutines that cooperatively respect cancellation contexts, implement depth-bounded Breadth-First-Search (BFS) traversal, provide graceful shutdown handling where workers drain remaining items upon cancellation, and build an ANSI live terminal dashboard reporting real-time crawl statistics. Finally, you will write a comprehensive integration test simulating an interconnected multi-page site graph.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Recursive Graph Deadlock Paradox & Dynamic Work Coordination
In traditional producer-consumer patterns, a fixed set of items is loaded into a buffered channel `jobs`, the channel is closed, and workers drain it. In a web crawler, workers are simultaneously consumers of jobs and producers of new child jobs. 

If workers attempt to write newly discovered URLs directly back into a bounded `jobs` channel:
- When the channel fills to capacity, all workers block waiting for channel buffer space.
- But no worker can drain the channel because all workers are blocked trying to write!
- Result: Instant, unrecoverable DEADLOCK (`fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!`).

To eliminate this hazard permanently, we introduce a **Master Coordinator Loop** with a decoupled Frontier Queue:
- Workers only communicate through two unidirectional channels: reading from `jobs <-chan CrawlJob` and writing to `results chan<- CrawlResult`.
- Workers **never** enqueue jobs directly.
- The master loop collects results from `results`, deduplicates newly discovered URLs, appends them to an unbounded in-memory frontier slice, and dispenses jobs into the `jobs` channel whenever a worker is idle.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        DEADLOCK-FREE WORKER COORDINATOR                                 |
|                                                                                         |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|               |                MASTER COORDINATOR LOOP                |                 |
|               |  - Unbounded In-Memory Frontier Queue [FIFO Slice]    |                 |
|               |  - Tracks inFlight counter (active jobs in workers)   |                 |
|               |  - Selects on: ctx.Done(), results channel, & jobs ch |                 |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|                         |                                   ^                           |
|            CrawlJob     |                                   |  CrawlResult              |
|            (URL, depth) |                                   |  (links, status)          |
|                         v                                   |                           |
|               +-------------------+               +-------------------+                 |
|               |   jobs channel    |               |  results channel  |                 |
|               |   (buffered: N)   |               |   (buffered: N)   |                 |
|               +-------------------+               +-------------------+                 |
|                         |                                   ^                           |
|          +--------------+--------------+                    |                           |
|          |              |              |                    |                           |
|          v              v              v                    |                           |
|     [Worker 1]     [Worker 2]     [Worker 3] ---------------+                           |
|     (Fetch HTML -> Parse Links -> Normalize -> Emit Result)                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Termination Condition: The In-Flight Counter Invariant
When does a web crawler know it has finished?
A common beginner bug is checking `if len(frontier) == 0 { return }`. This causes premature termination:
- The frontier may be empty momentarily because all 8 workers are currently downloading pages over the network.
- 50 milliseconds later, Worker 1 completes and discovers 20 new links.
- If the crawler exited when the frontier was empty, 90% of the website would be missed!

**The Termination Invariant**:
The crawler engine terminates if and only if:
$$\text{len}(\text{frontier}) == 0 \quad \text{AND} \quad \text{inFlight} == 0$$
Where $\text{inFlight}$ represents the exact count of tasks currently being processed by worker goroutines.

### 3. Breadth-First-Search (BFS) Depth-Bounded Traversal
Every `CrawlJob` carries a `Depth int` attribute:
- The initial seed URL has $\text{Depth} = 0$.
- Any link extracted from a page at $\text{Depth} = d$ produces a child task at $\text{Depth} = d + 1$.
- The coordinator enforces the maximum depth constraint: if $d + 1 > \text{MaxDepth}$, the link is discarded before enqueueing.

