# Phase 6 — Streaming JSONL Output, Performance Benchmarking & Multi-Platform Cross-Compilation

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Transform the crawler engine into a production-grade, distributable command-line tool. When crawling tens of thousands of web pages, storing results in in-memory slices before writing to disk leads to severe memory exhaustion (OOM) and total data loss if the process is interrupted. You will design and implement a streaming serialization engine supporting both Line-Delimited JSON (JSONL / NDJSON) and standard formatted JSON arrays. You will optimize disk I/O using buffered writers (`bufio.Writer`) with synchronized flushes that guarantee zero record loss upon receiving OS cancellation signals (`Ctrl+C`). You will embed Go runtime diagnostics with `net/http/pprof` for memory heap and goroutine leak profiling. Finally, you will construct a cross-compilation release matrix producing zero-dependency statically linked binaries for Linux, macOS (AMD64 and ARM64), and Windows stripped of debugging symbols (`-ldflags="-s -w"`) with binary footprints under 10 megabytes.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. JSON Lines (JSONL) vs Monolithic JSON Array Serialization
Writing a monolithic JSON array `[ { ... }, { ... } ]` presents severe scaling and resilience risks:
- **Memory Inefficiency**: All 50,000 page results must reside simultaneously in RAM before calling `json.Marshal(results)`. For rich HTML pages, this consumes gigabytes of heap memory.
- **Crash Fragility**: If the crawler crashes at page 49,999, the file lacks the closing `]` bracket, corrupting the entire output file and making it unparsable by standard JSON decoders.
- **In contrast, JSON Lines (JSONL / NDJSON)** emits one self-contained, newline-terminated JSON object per page immediately as it is crawled. If the process is halted prematurely, every previously written line remains 100% valid JSON and can be streamed incrementally into downstream analytics tools (`jq`, Python, Spark, or BigQuery).

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                    MONOLITHIC JSON BUFFER VS STREAMING JSON LINES                       |
|                                                                                         |
|  Approach A: Monolithic JSON Buffer (High Risk)                                         |
|  [ Crawl Page 1 ] ---> [ RAM Heap Slice ]                                               |
|  [ Crawl Page 2 ] ---> [ RAM Heap Slice ] ===> High Heap OOM Hazard                     |
|  [ Crawl Page N ] ---> [ RAM Heap Slice ]                                               |
|                               |                                                         |
|                               v                                                         |
|  At process finish: [ json.Marshal(slice) ] ===> Write [ {...}, {...} ]                 |
|  * IF CRASH OCCURS AT 99%: Entire output file corrupted with incomplete bracket!        |
|                                                                                         |
|  Approach B: Streaming JSON Lines with bufio (Our Architecture)                         |
|  [ Crawl Page 1 ] ===> [ bufio.Writer 64KB ] ===> Flush line 1 to disk: {"url": "..."}  |
|  [ Crawl Page 2 ] ===> [ bufio.Writer 64KB ] ===> Flush line 2 to disk: {"url": "..."}  |
|  * Low O(1) Memory Footprint: Constant RAM regardless of whether 100 or 1,000,000 pages |
|  * Complete Fault Tolerance: Every completed page is safely on disk immediately         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. High-Throughput Buffered Disk I/O with `bufio.Writer`
Invoking a kernel `write(2)` syscall for every individual crawled page causes heavy CPU context switching between user mode and kernel mode. By wrapping our output `*os.File` in a `bufio.NewWriterSize(file, 64*1024)` (64KB buffer), Go batches hundreds of serialized records into single block writes, boosting disk throughput by more than 10x.

A synchronized `Flush()` method ensures that any remaining bytes residing in the memory buffer are immediately flushed to permanent storage upon context cancellation.

### 3. Static Binary Cross-Compilation & Stripping Symbols (`-ldflags="-s -w"`)
Go's compiler (`go build`) provides native cross-compilation without external cross-toolchains:
- Setting `CGO_ENABLED=0` produces a completely standalone, statically linked executable with zero runtime dependencies on dynamic C libraries (`glibc`, `musl`). It runs seamlessly inside minimal Alpine Linux Docker containers, scratch containers, or bare-metal operating systems.
- Passing `-ldflags="-s -w"` instructs the linker to strip the symbol table (`-s`) and DWARF debug information (`-w`), reducing the compiled binary size from ~18MB down to ~7MB with zero performance penalty.

