# Phase 3 — Randomized Election Timers, Heartbeats & Split-Vote Prevention

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement Raft's Leader Election subsystem. In a Raft distributed cluster, nodes constantly exist in one of three roles: `Follower`, `Candidate`, or `Leader`. Followers remain passive and docile as long as they receive periodic heartbeats from a legitimate leader. When an election timer expires without receiving a heartbeat or granting a vote, the follower transitions to candidate, increments its `currentTerm`, votes for itself, and broadcasts `RequestVote` RPCs in parallel to all cluster peers. You will build a randomized election timer ($150\text{ms} - 300\text{ms}$) to avoid split-vote ties, implement the Log Up-to-Date rule (where a voter denies candidates whose log is less up-to-date than its own), enforce the Election Safety invariant (at most one leader per term), implement atomic majority quorum vote counting ($V > \lfloor N/2 \rfloor$), transition to Leader upon victory, broadcast immediate and periodic heartbeat `AppendEntries` RPCs (every 50ms), and handle higher-term discovery with immediate graceful demotion back to follower.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Raft Finite State Machine (FSM)
A Raft node continuously transitions between three operational states based on timers and consensus messages:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              RAFT NODE STATE TRANSITIONS                                |
|                                                                                         |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|               |                       FOLLOWER                        |                 |
|               |  - Responds to incoming RPCs from candidate & leader  |                 |
|               |  - Resets randomized election timer on valid msg     |                 |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|                      |                                       ^                          |
|                      | Election timeout elapsed              | Discovers higher term    |
|                      v                                       | or leader heartbeat      |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|               |                      CANDIDATE                        |                 |
|               |  - Increments currentTerm                             |                 |
|               |  - Votes for self                                     |                 |
|               |  - Broadcasts RequestVote to all peers                |                 |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|                      |                                       ^                          |
|                      | Wins election                         | Discovers higher term    |
|                      | (Votes > N/2)                         |                          |
|                      v                                       |                          |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
|               |                        LEADER                         |                 |
|               |  - Broadcasts heartbeats every 50ms                   |                 |
|               |  - Replicates client log commands                     |                 |
|               +-------------------------------------------------------+                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Randomized Election Timeouts & Split-Vote Livelock Prevention
If all cluster nodes used a deterministic, synchronized election timeout (e.g. exactly 200ms):
1. The leader fails.
2. All $N$ followers time out simultaneously.
3. Each follower increments its term and votes for itself.
4. Each node requests votes from the others, but since all have already voted for themselves, no node obtains a majority quorum.
5. The election splits. All timers expire again at the same time, producing an endless split-vote livelock where the cluster can never elect a leader.

**The Solution: Randomized Timeouts**:
$$T_{\text{election}} \sim \mathcal{U}(T_{\text{min}}, T_{\text{max}}), \quad \text{where } T_{\text{min}} = 150\text{ms}, \; T_{\text{max}} = 300\text{ms}$$
By drawing $T_{\text{election}}$ uniformly from a range that is wide relative to the network broadcast time ($T_{\text{broadcast}} \approx 5\text{ms}$), one candidate will almost always time out before its peers, gather majority votes, and assert authority before other nodes time out.

### 3. The Log Up-to-Date Rule: Leader Completeness Invariant
Raft enforces a critical safety property called **Leader Completeness**: if a log entry is committed in a given term, then that entry will be present in the logs of the leaders for all higher terms. To guarantee this without transferring logs backwards from followers, Raft places an eligibility restriction on voting:

**Log Up-to-Date Check**:
A voter node $V$ with last log entry $(T_V, I_V)$ denies its vote to Candidate $C$ with last log entry $(T_C, I_C)$ unless Candidate $C$'s log is at least as up-to-date as $V$'s log:
$$\text{Granted} \iff (T_C > T_V) \lor (T_C = T_V \land I_C \ge I_V)$$
Because a committed entry must be present on a majority of servers, and an elected candidate must gather votes from a majority of servers, the candidate's majority and the committed entry's majority must overlap on at least one node. That node will deny its vote to any candidate lacking the committed entry, ensuring only candidates with complete logs can win elections.

