# Phase 1 — Raw Packet Capture Engine, Interface Probing & Linux Security Capabilities

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Construct the raw packet ingestion subsystem, device enumeration engine, and privilege separation architecture for a high-performance network sniffer in Rust. In standard operating system networking, the Network Interface Card (NIC) hardware filter discards any Ethernet frame whose destination MAC address does not match the local host or a multicast/broadcast address. To capture all network traffic flowing across the physical segment (like Wireshark or tcpdump), the interface must be configured in **Promiscuous Mode**.

You will build a device probing engine that scans available network interfaces (Ethernet, Wi-Fi, loopback, virtual bridges), queries their IPv4/IPv6 addresses and hardware MAC addresses, and presents an interactive interface selector. You will initialize the underlying kernel packet capture socket using Rust bindings to `libpcap`, configuring optimal capture parameters: full frame snapshot length (`snaplen = 65535`), 4MB kernel ring buffer to prevent buffer overruns, and a 100ms packet read timeout to keep threads responsive. Finally, you will eliminate the security anti-pattern of running the sniffer as `root` by configuring granular Linux POSIX capabilities (`CAP_NET_RAW` and `CAP_NET_ADMIN`) via `setcap`, and author integration tests verifying packet reception.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The Kernel Packet Ingestion Pipeline & Promiscuous Mode
When an Ethernet frame hits the physical wire, it travels through several hardware and kernel stages before reaching user space:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        KERNEL PACKET INGESTION & PROMISCUOUS FLOW                       |
|                                                                                         |
|   Physical Wire / Radio Waves                                                           |
|               |                                                                         |
|               v                                                                         |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
|   | Network Interface Card (NIC) Hardware |                                             |
|   | - Promiscuous Mode = DISABLED: Discards frames not addressed to our MAC.             |
|   | - Promiscuous Mode = ENABLED:  Passes ALL frames to OS kernel ring buffer!          |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
|               |                                                                         |
|               v                                                                         |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
|   | Linux Kernel AF_PACKET / BPF Buffer   | <--- Ring Buffer Size = 4MB                 |
|   | - Stores raw frames with timestamps   |                                             |
|   | - If user space lags: PACKET DROPS!   |                                             |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
|               |                                                                         |
|               | Zero-copy / poll() with 100ms timeout                                   |
|               v                                                                         |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
|   | Rust User Space Application (sniff)   |                                             |
|   | - libpcap wrapper (pcap crate)        |                                             |
|   | - PacketHeader: ts, caplen, len       |                                             |
|   | - PacketData: raw &[u8] bytes slice   |                                             |
|   +---------------------------------------+                                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**The Kernel Buffer Overrun Formula**:
$$\text{Drop Rate} = \frac{\text{Dropped Packets}}{\text{Total Packets Received}} \times 100\%$$
If packet arrival rate $\lambda$ exceeds user-space consumption rate $\mu$ for longer than $T = \frac{\text{Buffer Size}}{\lambda - \mu}$, the kernel drops newly arrived packets at the socket level. A 4MB ring buffer combined with a non-blocking consumer thread prevents drops during traffic bursts.

### 2. Privilege Separation: POSIX Capabilities vs Running as Root
Running network analyzers as `root` (`sudo ./sniff`) exposes the entire host to catastrophic vulnerabilities if a buffer overflow occurs inside a parser. Linux provides fine-grained capability partitioning:
- **`CAP_NET_RAW`**: Permits opening `AF_PACKET` raw sockets to read raw frames off the wire.
- **`CAP_NET_ADMIN`**: Permits modifying interface flags (enabling promiscuous mode `IFF_PROMISC`).

Command formulation:
```bash
sudo setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin=eip target/release/sniff
```
This enables unprivileged execution while satisfying all kernel security checks.

### 3. Snapshot Length (`snaplen`) & Read Timeout
- **`snaplen = 65535`**: Captures full Ethernet jumbo frames (up to 9000 bytes) and standard MTU frames (1514 bytes) without truncating upper-layer application payloads.
- **`timeout = 100ms`**: In `libpcap`, a timeout of `0` blocks indefinitely until a packet arrives. Setting a 100ms timeout allows the capture thread loop to periodically check termination signals (`AtomicBool`), enabling responsive, graceful shutdown on Ctrl+C.

