# Phase 2 — Zero-Copy Ethernet Frame & IPv4 Protocol Parsing with Lifetime Views

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Design and implement a zero-copy binary protocol parser for Layer 2 (Ethernet II) and Layer 3 (IPv4) network packets. When sniffing a Gigabit or 10-Gigabit link, an analyzer must process up to $1.488 \times 10^6$ packets per second. If the parser heap-allocates structs or vectors for every arriving frame, memory allocation latency and garbage collection/drop overhead will immediately saturate the CPU, triggering massive kernel buffer drops.

You will build a zero-copy parsing pipeline utilizing Rust lifetimes (`&'a [u8]`). Instead of copying bytes out of the capture buffer, your parser creates lightweight view structs that borrow contiguous slices directly from the packet buffer. You will decode Ethernet II headers (Source MAC, Destination MAC, EtherType), handle 802.1Q VLAN encapsulation tags, decode full IPv4 headers (handling variable-length IP Options via the Internet Header Length / IHL field), convert multi-byte integers from Network Byte Order (Big Endian) to host endianness, verify the RFC 1071 one's-complement Internet Header Checksum, and author unit tests validating parser accuracy against raw hex fixtures captured in Wireshark.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Zero-Copy Lifetime Architecture
In Rust, lifetimes allow safe, pointer-based views over memory without allocations:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        ZERO-COPY BORROWED SLICE ARCHITECTURE                            |
|                                                                                         |
|  Raw Packet Buffer in Memory (&'a [u8], len = 1514):                                    |
|  [0 .............. 13][14 ..................... 33][34 ........................... 1513]|
|  |-- Ethernet II ---||------- IPv4 Header --------||------- Transport / Payload -------||
|                                                                                         |
|  Zero-Copy View Structs:                                                                |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | EthernetFrame<'a> { raw: &'a [u8][0..14] }                                        |  |
|  | - dst_mac: [u8; 6]                                                                |  |
|  | - src_mac: [u8; 6]                                                                |  |
|  | - ethertype: u16                                                                  |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                            |                                                            |
|                            | .payload() returns slice [14..1514]                        |
|                            v                                                            |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Ipv4Packet<'a> { raw: &'a [u8][14..1514] }                                        |  |
|  | - version: 4, ihl: 5 (20 bytes header)                                            |  |
|  | - src_ip: 192.168.1.50, dst_ip: 142.250.190.46                                    |  |
|  | - protocol: 6 (TCP)                                                               |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                            |                                                            |
|                            | .payload() returns slice [34..1514]                        |
|                            v                                                            |
|                     Transport Layer Parser (TCP / UDP)                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Binary Header Layout & Bitfield Extraction
Network headers encode multiple fields into packed byte bitfields. For example, byte 0 of an IPv4 header contains:

```
  0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7
 +-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+
 |Version|  IHL  |
 +-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+
```

**Mathematical Extraction Formulas**:
$$\text{Version} = b[0] \gg 4$$
$$\text{IHL (Internet Header Length in 32-bit words)} = b[0] \ \&\ 0x0F$$
$$\text{Header Length in Bytes} = \text{IHL} \times 4$$

Bounds check invariant: Any valid IPv4 packet must satisfy:
$$\text{Total Packet Length} \ge 20 \quad \land \quad \text{Version} = 4 \quad \land \quad \text{IHL} \ge 5 \quad \land \quad \text{Total Packet Length} \ge \text{IHL} \times 4$$

### 3. Network Byte Order (Big Endian) vs Host Endianness
Internet protocols transmit the most significant byte first (Big Endian). On x86_64 host hardware (Little Endian), interpreting multi-byte integers directly causes inverted values. We perform zero-cost register endianness swaps using `u16::from_be_bytes`:
$$\text{Wire: } [0x08, 0x00] \xrightarrow{\text{u16::from_be_bytes}} 0x0800 \quad (\text{IPv4 EtherType})$$

### 4. RFC 1071 One's-Complement Internet Checksum
The IPv4 header checksum is the 16-bit one's complement of the one's complement sum of all 16-bit words in the header. Summing all 16-bit words of a valid header (including the checksum field itself) results in `0xFFFF`.

