# Phase 3 — Transport Layer Demuxing: TCP State Flags, Sequence Tracking & UDP Datagrams

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement the Layer 4 (Transport Layer) protocol demultiplexing subsystem for TCP, UDP, and ICMP. Once the Ethernet frame and IPv4 network headers have been stripped and validated, the application must inspect the IPv4 `protocol` field to determine which transport decoder to invoke: Protocol `6` for Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), Protocol `17` for User Datagram Protocol (UDP), or Protocol `1` for Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP).

You will build zero-copy borrowed parsers for TCP segments and UDP datagrams. For TCP, you will parse the 16-bit Source and Destination ports, 32-bit Sequence numbers, 32-bit Acknowledgment numbers, calculate dynamic TCP header length using the Data Offset (DOFF) bitfield to correctly bypass variable TCP options (such as MSS, Timestamps, and SACK), and model TCP control flags (SYN, ACK, FIN, RST, PSH, URG, ECE, CWR) using the type-safe `bitflags` crate. For UDP, you will decode the 8-byte fixed header and isolate the user payload slice. For ICMP, you will decode control message types (Echo Request/Reply, Destination Unreachable). Finally, you will construct a unified `TransportPacket` algebraic enum and verify transport decoding against captured packet fixtures.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Transport Layer Protocol Demultiplexing
IPv4 packets carry diverse transport protocols distinguished by the 8-bit `protocol` header byte:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        TRANSPORT LAYER DEMULTIPLEXING ARCHITECTURE                      |
|                                                                                         |
|  IPv4 Packet (Protocol Field: raw[9]):                                                  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Protocol = 6 (TCP)            | Protocol = 17 (UDP)          | Protocol = 1 (ICMP)|  |
|  +-------------------------------+------------------------------+--------------------+  |
|                 |                               |                         |             |
|                 v                               v                         v             |
|  +-----------------------------+ +----------------------------+ +---------------------+ |
|  | TcpSegment<'a>              | | UdpDatagram<'a>            | | IcmpMessage<'a>     | |
|  | - src_port: u16             | | - src_port: u16            | | - msg_type: u8      | |
|  | - dst_port: u16             | | - dst_port: u16            | | - code: u8          | |
|  | - seq_num: u32              | | - length: u16              | | - checksum: u16     | |
|  | - ack_num: u32              | | - checksum: u16            | | - payload: &'a [u8] | |
|  | - data_offset: usize        | | - payload: &'a [u8]        | +---------------------+ |
|  | - flags: TcpFlags           | +----------------------------+                         |
|  | - window_size: u16          |                                                        |
|  | - payload: &'a [u8]         |                                                        |
|  +-----------------------------+                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. TCP Header Structure & Data Offset (DOFF)
A standard TCP segment header is between 20 and 60 bytes in length. Byte 12 encodes the **Data Offset** in its upper 4 bits:

```
  0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7
 +-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+
 |DataOff| Rsrvd |
 +-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+
```

**Mathematical Invariant**:
$$\text{Data Offset (Words)} = b[12] \gg 4$$
$$\text{TCP Header Length in Bytes} = \text{Data Offset} \times 4$$
$$\text{Payload Slice} = \text{raw}[\text{TCP Header Length} \dots]$$

If a parser hardcodes 20 bytes for TCP, any segment containing TCP Options (such as Window Scale `0x03` or Timestamps `0x08`) will misinterpret options bytes as application payload data, corrupting HTTP, TLS, or DNS decoders!

### 3. TCP Control Flags Representation (`bitflags`)
Byte 13 contains the core TCP state machine control flags:

| Bit Position | Flag Name | Semantic Meaning in TCP State Machine |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `0x01` | **FIN** | No more data from sender; initiates connection teardown |
| `0x02` | **SYN** | Synchronize sequence numbers; initiates 3-way handshake |
| `0x04` | **RST** | Reset the connection immediately (abrupt termination) |
| `0x08` | **PSH** | Push function; receiver should pass buffered data to app immediately |
| `0x10` | **ACK** | Acknowledgment field is significant |
| `0x20` | **URG** | Urgent pointer field is significant |
| `0x40` | **ECE** | ECN-Echo (congestion notification from network router) |
| `0x80` | **CWR** | Congestion Window Reduced (sender reduced sending window) |

