# Phase 4 — Bidirectional Flow Aggregation, 5-Tuple Normalization & In-Kernel BPF Filtering

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Transform isolated, raw packet events into stateful, bidirectional network communication streams (Flows) and implement in-kernel packet filtering. In high-speed network analysis, inspecting individual packets in isolation fails to capture session context: a client downloading a 100MB file produces 70,000 discrete packets. Analysts and security detection systems require aggregated **Flow Records** that summarize the total conversation between two network endpoints.

You will build a stateful Flow Table engine in Rust. You will implement canonical **5-Tuple Normalization** (`Source IP`, `Source Port`, `Destination IP`, `Destination Port`, `Protocol`) so that forward packets (Client $\to$ Server) and reverse reply packets (Server $\to$ Client) map to the exact same hash table entry. You will track directional metrics (packets sent, bytes transferred, connection start and last-seen timestamps, session duration), track TCP connection state transitions (`SYN`, `ESTABLISHED`, `FIN`, `RST`), push packet filtering into the kernel using **Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF)** bytecode (such as `tcp port 443 or udp port 53`) to eliminate user-space copy overhead for discarded packets, and implement background eviction for expired idle flows.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Canonical 5-Tuple Normalization
A network conversation between Host A and Host B is bidirectional. Packets transmitted from $A \to B$ and responses from $B \to A$ belong to the same session. A naive `HashMap` keyed by `(src_ip, src_port, dst_ip, dst_port)` would split this conversation into two separate unilateral flows.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        BIDIRECTIONAL 5-TUPLE NORMALIZATION                              |
|                                                                                         |
|  Forward Packet (Client -> Server):                                                     |
|  Src: 192.168.1.50:54321, Dst: 142.250.190.46:443, Proto: TCP                          |
|                                                                                         |
|  Reverse Packet (Server -> Client):                                                     |
|  Src: 142.250.190.46:443, Dst: 192.168.1.50:54321, Proto: TCP                          |
|                                                                                         |
|                                   Canonical Normalization                               |
|                                              |                                          |
|                                              v                                          |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | FlowKey:                                                                          |  |
|  |   endpoint_a: (142.250.190.46, 443)    <--- Lexicographically Smaller             |  |
|  |   endpoint_b: (192.168.1.50, 54321)    <--- Lexicographically Larger              |  |
|  |   protocol:   6 (TCP)                                                             |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                              |                                          |
|                                              v                                          |
|  Single Shared Entry in FlowTable:                                                      |
|  - forward_packets: 142,  forward_bytes: 18,240                                         |
|  - reverse_packets: 230,  reverse_bytes: 284,910                                        |
|  - tcp_state: Established, duration: 1.42s                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**Mathematical Ordering Rule**:
$$\text{Endpoint}(A) = (\text{IP}_{src}, \text{Port}_{src}), \quad \text{Endpoint}(B) = (\text{IP}_{dst}, \text{Port}_{dst})$$
$$\text{Endpoint}_1 = \min(A, B), \quad \text{Endpoint}_2 = \max(A, B)$$
$$\text{FlowKey} = (\text{Endpoint}_1, \text{Endpoint}_2, \text{Protocol})$$

### 2. In-Kernel BPF Filtering vs User-Space Discard
When capturing on a saturated 1 Gbps link, copying every raw packet across the kernel boundary into user space consumes massive CPU memory bandwidth ($125\text{ MB/sec}$). If the analyst only cares about DNS traffic, filtering in user space forces the application to copy and decode millions of irrelevant packets before discarding them.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                            KERNEL BPF FILTERING ADVANTAGE                               |
|                                                                                         |
|  Physical Wire (1 Gbps Mixed Traffic)                                                   |
|         |                                                                               |
|         v                                                                               |
|  [ NIC Hardware Ring Buffer ]                                                           |
|         |                                                                               |
|         v                                                                               |
|  [ Linux Kernel BPF Engine ] <--- filter: "tcp port 443 or udp port 53"                |
|         |                                 |                                             |
|         | (Non-Matching: 95% of packets)  | (Matching: 5% of packets)                   |
|         v                                 v                                             |
|      DISCARDED!                  Copied to AF_PACKET Buffer                             |
|   (0 user CPU cycles)                     |                                             |
|                                           v                                             |
|                                   Rust Sniffer Process                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```
`pcap::Capture::filter(&mut self, "expr", true)` compiles the filter string into BPF bytecode, loading it into the kernel socket via the `SO_ATTACH_FILTER` socket option.

### 3. TCP Connection State Tracking
Flows track connection lifecycles based on TCP flags:
- **`SynSent`**: Initial `SYN` observed.
- **`SynReceived`**: `SYN-ACK` observed in opposite direction.
- **`Established`**: `ACK` completes three-way handshake.
- **`Closing`**: `FIN` observed from either endpoint.
- **`Closed`**: `FIN` acknowledged or `RST` received.

