# Phase 6 — Offline PCAP Replay, File Export, Ring Dumps & Comprehensive End-to-End Test Suite

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Equip your network analyzer with enterprise-grade `.pcap` capture file export and offline replay capabilities, and construct an automated end-to-end integration test suite. In real-world incident response and performance auditing, live terminal monitoring is only the first step: security and network engineers require the ability to dump live packet captures to disk in standard `libpcap` format so files can be opened in Wireshark, ingested by intrusion detection systems (Snort, Suricata, Zeek), or shared across teams for post-mortem forensics.

Furthermore, automated CI/CD test runners (such as GitHub Actions) execute in sandboxed Linux containers where raw socket access (`AF_PACKET`) is strictly forbidden by container security profiles. You will implement an offline file capture engine (`pcap::Capture::from_file`) that reads recorded `.pcap` streams at high speed without requiring physical NICs or elevated privileges. You will implement live file export (`-w / --write output.pcap`) using `pcap::Savefile`, unpack the low-level binary layout of PCAP files (the 24-byte Global File Header and 16-byte Packet Headers), construct synthetic test fixtures, and author end-to-end regression tests verifying that flow reconstruction and protocol demuxing yield identical results across live and recorded streams.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Binary PCAP File Format Specification
A standard `.pcap` capture file begins with a 24-byte Global Header followed by consecutive packet records (each prefixed by a 16-byte Packet Header):

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              BINARY PCAP FILE FORMAT SPECIFICATION                      |
|                                                                                         |
|  GLOBAL FILE HEADER (24 Bytes):                                                         |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Magic (4B) | Ver Maj (2B) | Ver Min (2B) | ThisZone (4B) | SigFigs (4B) | SnapLen |  |
|  | 0xa1b2c3d4 | 2            | 4            | 0             | 0            | 65535   |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Link-Layer Header Type (4B): 1 = DLT_EN10MB (Standard 10/100/1000 Mbps Ethernet)   |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                                                                         |
|  PACKET RECORD 1:                                                                       |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | ts_sec (4B) | ts_usec (4B) | caplen (4B) | orig_len (4B) | Raw Frame Bytes (caplen)|  |
|  | Epoch Secs  | Microseconds | e.g. 54     | e.g. 54       | Ethernet + IP + TCP     |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                                                                         |
|  PACKET RECORD 2:                                                                       |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | ts_sec (4B) | ts_usec (4B) | caplen (4B) | orig_len (4B) | Raw Frame Bytes (caplen)|  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**The Endianness Magic Invariant**:
- `0xa1b2c3d4`: Native byte order file (microsecond resolution).
- `0xd4c3b2a1`: Swapped byte order (file written on Little Endian, read on Big Endian).
- `0xa1b23c4d`: Nanosecond timestamp resolution (`pcap-ng` precursor).

### 2. Dual-Engine Capture Source Abstraction
We encapsulate live network interface capturing and offline file playback under a unified `CaptureSource` abstraction:

```rust
pub enum CaptureSource {
    Live(pcap::Capture<pcap::Active>),
    Offline(pcap::Capture<pcap::Offline>),
}
```
Both implement a unified `next_packet()` method, enabling identical downstream decoding, flow aggregation, and UI display logic.

### 3. Automated CI/CD Testing Without Elevated Privileges
By decoupling protocol analysis from live network interfaces:
1. Integration tests invoke `Capture::from_file("tests/fixtures/traffic.pcap")`.
2. Tests execute completely in unprivileged user space without `sudo` or `setcap`.
3. Tests produce deterministic assertions (exact packet counts, known byte totals, expected IP endpoints) with zero flakiness.

