# Phase 1 — Write-Ahead Log (WAL), Checksummed Binary Framing & Durability Guarantees

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Construct the crash-resilient Write-Ahead Log (WAL) subsystem for a high-performance Log-Structured Merge (LSM) Tree storage engine in Rust. In an LSM-tree (the storage architecture powering RocksDB, LevelDB, and Cassandra), high write throughput is achieved by buffering new keys in an in-memory `MemTable`. However, volatile RAM loses all mutations upon sudden power failure, operating system panic, or process crash. To guarantee the **Durability** property of ACID without sacrificing write performance, every mutation (`Put` or `Delete`) must be appended sequentially to an append-only disk log before updating in-memory data structures.

You will build a zero-allocation binary framing protocol that packages key-value operations with CRC32 checksums using `crc32fast`. You will implement torn-write detection and recovery: if a crash strikes mid-write, partial disk writes are detected by checksum verification, allowing the storage engine to truncate corrupted trailing bytes and safely recover all acknowledged mutations. You will implement configurable hardware disk flushing policies using `fdatasync` (`File::sync_data()`), and author automated fault-injection test suites that simulate power loss and bit-flip corruptions.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Binary Record Framing Format & Checksum Protection
Every WAL entry is framed with a fixed 8-byte header followed by variable-length payload bytes:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                 BINARY WAL RECORD FORMAT                                |
|                                                                                         |
|  Fixed Record Header (8 Bytes):                                                         |
|  +-------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Payload Length (4 Bytes, Little-End)| CRC32 Checksum (4 Bytes, Little-Endian)     |  |
|  | u32: KeyLen + ValLen + 9 bytes      | crc32fast::hash(&payload)                   |  |
|  +-------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+  |
|                                                                                         |
|  Variable Record Payload:                                                               |
|  +---------------+---------------------+--------------------+------------+------------+  |
|  | OpType (1B)   | Key Length (4B, LE) | Val Length (4B, LE)| Key Bytes  | Val Bytes  |  |
|  | 0=Put, 1=Del  | u32                 | u32 (0 for delete) | raw &[u8]  | raw &[u8]  |  |
|  +---------------+---------------------+--------------------+------------+------------+  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**The Torn-Write Invariant**:
$$\text{Record Valid} \iff \text{BytesRead} = \text{PayloadLen} \quad \land \quad \text{crc32fast}(\text{Payload}) = \text{HeaderCRC}$$
If power fails while the disk controller writes a sector, the tail of the WAL file may contain a partial record. The WAL replay engine checks both length bounds and CRC32: if the file ends prematurely or the checksum fails at the end of the file, replay gracefully halts and reports the exact byte offset where valid mutations ended.

### 2. Disk Synchronization: `sync_data()` vs `sync_all()`
In Linux/POSIX file systems, `File::sync_all()` executes `fsync(2)`, which flushes both the file data blocks and the file inode metadata (modification time, permissions) to physical storage. In contrast, `File::sync_data()` executes `fdatasync(2)`, which flushes only the modified data blocks and file length, avoiding unnecessary disk head movement to update inode access timestamps. In append-heavy workloads, `fdatasync` provides up to a **2x improvement in write IOPS** while offering identical durability.

### 3. Buffered Appending vs Durability Mode
We support two durability modes:
- **`SyncMode::Immediate`**: Calls `BufWriter::flush()` followed by `File::sync_data()` on every individual `put` or `delete`. Guarantees Zero Recovery Point Objective (RPO=0) at the cost of disk I/O latency.
- **`SyncMode::Buffered`**: Buffers writes in memory and flushes periodically (e.g. every 10ms or when the 64KB buffer fills). Yields $>150,000$ operations/sec with bounded microsecond loss windows.

