# Phase 2 — In-Memory MemTable, SkipList / BTreeMap, Tombstones & Flush Thresholds

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Design and construct the high-speed in-memory write buffer (**MemTable**) for your LSM-tree storage engine. In an LSM storage engine, the MemTable serves two vital functions: it provides sub-microsecond $O(\log N)$ point lookups and range scans for recently written keys, and it maintains mutations in lexicographically sorted order so that when memory fills up, the engine can stream a sequential, sorted disk file (**SSTable**) without performing expensive disk sorting passes.

You will build an in-memory MemTable using Rust's `BTreeMap` parameterized over raw byte slices (`Vec<u8>`). You will model deletions using explicit **Tombstones** (`Option::None`), which shadow older values on disk until background compaction purges them. You will implement precise byte-level memory tracking to accurately detect when the MemTable exceeds its configured capacity (e.g. 4MB or 64MB). You will construct an active-to-frozen MemTable rotation engine using `Arc` and `parking_lot::RwLock`, allowing reader threads to query both active and frozen tables concurrently without blocking incoming writes, and author unit tests validating sorted iteration, tombstone shadowing, and flush triggering.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. MemTable Lifecycle: Active Mutable vs Frozen Immutable
To prevent writes from stalling during disk I/O, the storage engine decouples write ingestion from disk flushing using dual MemTable slots:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        MEMTABLE ROTATION & FLUSH ARCHITECTURE                           |
|                                                                                         |
|  Client Writes (Put / Delete)                                                           |
|         |                                                                               |
|         v                                                                               |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Active Mutable MemTable (BTreeMap)                                                |  |
|  | - Capacity Threshold = 4MB                                                        |  |
|  | - Current Size: 4,194,310 Bytes -> FLUSH THRESHOLD REACHED!                        |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|         |                                                                               |
|         | Freeze Active -> Move reference to Immutable Slot                             |
|         v                                                                               |
|  +---------------------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+ |
|  | Frozen Immutable MemTable (Arc<MemTable>)         |   | New Active MemTable        | |
|  | - Read-Only (Serves Get queries)                  |   | - Accepts incoming Writes  | |
|  | - Dispatched to Background SSTable Flush Thread!  |   | - Memory Counter Reset: 0B | |
|  +---------------------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+ |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Tombstone Semantics & Value Shadowing
In traditional B-Tree databases (such as PostgreSQL or SQLite), deleting a key directly modifies a leaf page in-place. In an append-only LSM-tree, in-place disk overwrites are strictly prohibited. A deletion is recorded by inserting a **Tombstone** (`Option::None`):
- When a client executes `delete("user:1001")`, a tombstone entry is placed into the active MemTable.
- During reads, search order is strictly: $\text{Active MemTable} \to \text{Frozen MemTable} \to \text{SSTables (Newest to Oldest)}$.
- The first matching key terminates the lookup. If the match is a tombstone, the engine returns `Ok(None)` (Key Not Found), successfully masking all older versions of that key residing on disk.

### 3. Precise Byte-Level Memory Accounting
Accurately tracking MemTable size prevents out-of-memory (OOM) crashes under heavy write pressure:
$$\text{Node Overhead} \approx 32 \text{ bytes (BTree internal pointers)}$$
$$\text{Inserted Size} = \text{len}(key) + \text{len}(value) + 32$$
If an existing key is updated, the change in memory footprint is:
$$\Delta \text{Size} = \text{len}(new\_value) - \text{len}(old\_value)$$
When $\sum \text{Size} \ge \text{Threshold}$, the MemTable signals a flush condition.

