# Phase 4 — Probabilistic Bloom Filters, False Positive Math & Multi-Level Read Path

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement high-performance probabilistic **Bloom Filters** and build the unified multi-layered read path for your LSM storage engine. In an LSM-tree with dozens of persistent SSTables on disk, reading a non-existent key (or a cold key) creates catastrophic read amplification: without optimization, the engine must open every single SSTable and perform random disk reads on each file only to discover the key does not exist.

You will build a space-efficient Bloom filter in Rust that guarantees **zero false negatives**: if the filter reports a key is absent, the key is guaranteed $100\%$ not present in that SSTable, completely eliminating disk I/O for that file. You will implement the **Kirsch-Mitzenmacher Double-Hashing** algorithm to derive $k$ independent hash probes from just two 64-bit hashes, serialize the bit array into the SSTable file format, and architect the unified layered read path: checking Active MemTable $\to$ Frozen MemTable $\to$ SSTables in reverse chronological order (newest to oldest), halting immediately on the first match (whether a live value or a tombstone), and authoring calibration tests verifying that false positive rates stay below $1\%$.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Bloom Filter Mathematical Formulations
A Bloom filter is a bit array of $m$ bits initialized to 0, parameterized by $k$ independent hash functions. For $n$ elements with desired false positive probability $p$:

**Optimal Bit Array Size ($m$)**:
$$m = -\frac{n \cdot \ln(p)}{(\ln 2)^2} \approx -1.442695 \cdot n \cdot \log_2(p)$$

**Optimal Number of Hash Probes ($k$)**:
$$k = \frac{m}{n} \cdot \ln 2 \approx 0.693147 \cdot \frac{m}{n}$$

For $p = 0.01$ (1% false positive probability):
$$\frac{m}{n} \approx 9.585 \implies \approx 10 \text{ bits per key}, \quad k = 10 \times 0.693 \approx 7 \text{ hash functions}$$
Each key requires only **1.25 bytes of memory**, yet avoids $99\%$ of unnecessary disk reads!

### 2. Kirsch-Mitzenmacher Double-Hashing Optimization
Running 7 distinct cryptographic hashing functions per key introduces substantial CPU overhead. Kirsch and Mitzenmacher proved that two independent hash values ($h_1$ and $h_2$) can simulate $k$ independent hash functions without degrading false positive bounds:
$$g_i(x) = (h_1(x) + i \cdot h_2(x)) \pmod m \quad \text{for } i \in [0, k-1]$$

### 3. Layered Read Path Hierarchy & Short-Circuit Execution

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         LAYERED READ PATH SHORT-CIRCUIT FLOW                            |
|                                                                                         |
|  Client Query: get(key)                                                                 |
|         |                                                                               |
|         v                                                                               |
|  [ Step 1: Active MemTable ] ---- (Found Value) ----> Return Ok(Some(value))            |
|         |                   ---- (Found Tombstone) -> Return Ok(None) [Short-Circuit!]  |
|         | (Miss)                                                                        |
|         v                                                                               |
|  [ Step 2: Frozen MemTable ] ---- (Found Value) ----> Return Ok(Some(value))            |
|         |                   ---- (Found Tombstone) -> Return Ok(None) [Short-Circuit!]  |
|         | (Miss)                                                                        |
|         v                                                                               |
|  [ Step 3: SSTables Loop (Newest -> Oldest) ]                                           |
|         |                                                                               |
|         +---> Check SSTable_N Bloom Filter in RAM                                       |
|                  |                                                                      |
|                  +-- Filter says NO (Negative) ---> 0 DISK READS! Continue to N-1.      |
|                  |                                                                      |
|                  +-- Filter says MAYBE (Positive)                                       |
|                           |                                                             |
|                           v                                                             |
|                      Binary Search Sparse Index & Read 4KB Block from Disk              |
|                           |                                                             |
|                           +-- Match Found?                                              |
|                                 +-- Value: Return Ok(Some(val)) [Done!]                 |
|                                 +-- Tombstone: Return Ok(None) [Short-Circuit!]         |
|                                 +-- False Positive: Continue Loop to N-1                |
|         |                                                                               |
|  All Tables Exhausted -> Return Ok(None) (Key Does Not Exist)                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

