# Phase 5 — K-Way Merge Compaction, Tombstone Purging & Space Amplification Control

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement the background garbage collection engine of your LSM-tree: **K-Way Merge Compaction**. Because an LSM storage engine writes all mutations and deletions as sequential append-only logs, multiple immutable SSTables accumulate on disk over time. Without compaction, two fatal issues degrade system health: **Space Amplification** (disk usage balloons indefinitely as obsolete versions of updated keys and deleted tombstones remain on disk) and **Read Amplification** (point lookups and range queries must inspect an ever-increasing number of SSTables, degrading read throughput).

You will build a streaming multi-file K-Way merge engine using `std::collections::BinaryHeap` (Min-Heap) that merges $K$ sorted SSTables in $O(N \log K)$ time. You will resolve duplicate keys deterministically: when the same key appears in multiple SSTables, the version from the newer SSTable takes precedence while older superseded versions are discarded. You will implement the **Bottom-Level Tombstone Invariant**: a deletion tombstone is preserved if older SSTables exist beneath the compaction tier, but is permanently purged if compacting into the bottom-most level. Finally, you will implement atomic file replacement to ensure crash consistency during compaction and author unit tests validating space reclamation and data integrity.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. K-Way Merge with Min-Heap Priority Queue
Compacting $K$ sorted files requires finding the globally smallest key across all files at each iteration step:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                             K-WAY MERGE COMPACTION ARCHITECTURE                         |
|                                                                                         |
|  Input SSTables:                                                                        |
|  - SSTable 1 (Newest, Age 0): [k1:v1_new,  k3:v3_new,  k4:TOMBSTONE]                   |
|  - SSTable 2 (Older,  Age 1): [k1:v1_old,  k2:v2,      k3:v3_old]                       |
|  - SSTable 3 (Oldest, Age 2): [k1:v1_vold, k4:v4_old,  k5:v5]                           |
|                                                                                         |
|  Min-Heap Priority Queue (Sorted by (Key ASC, Age ASC)):                                |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Heap extracts smallest Key across all iterators.                                  |  |
|  | When identical keys tie:                                                          |  |
|  | -> Retain version with lowest Age (Newest SSTable).                                |  |
|  | -> Silently discard older duplicates!                                             |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                    |                                                    |
|                                    v                                                    |
|  Compacted Output SSTable:                                                              |
|  - k1:v1_new     (superseded older versions dropped)                                    |
|  - k2:v2                                                                                |
|  - k3:v3_new     (superseded older versions dropped)                                    |
|  - k4: (Tombstone purged if bottom level! Otherwise written as tombstone)               |
|  - k5:v5                                                                                |
|                                                                                         |
|  Atomic Switch: Delete SSTable 1, 2, 3 -> Register Compacted SSTable 4                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. The Bottom-Level Tombstone Purging Invariant
Tombstones cannot simply be thrown away during compaction:
$$\text{Can Purge Tombstone}(K) \iff \text{Compacting into Bottom-Most Storage Tier}$$
- If an SSTable exists below the compaction level containing an older version ($K: V_{old}$), purging the tombstone now would cause $V_{old}$ to magically **resurrect** on future queries!
- A tombstone must be retained until it reaches the bottom-most level where no older versions of $K$ exist anywhere on disk.

### 3. Streaming Bounded-Memory Compaction
Compacting two 10GB SSTables into a single file must NOT load 20GB into RAM. By streaming 4KB data blocks from each input table on-demand, memory consumption is strictly bounded:
$$\text{RAM Footprint} = O(K \cdot \text{Block Size}) = O(K \cdot 4\text{KB})$$
Compacting 10 SSTables requires less than **1MB of RAM**, regardless of total file sizes.

