# Phase 6 — Public Engine API, Manifest Metadata & Chaos Crash-Recovery Test Suite

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Package the complete LSM storage engine into a production-grade, thread-safe embedded key-value database library (`LsmDb`) and construct an automated chaos crash-recovery test harness. In enterprise storage systems (such as RocksDB and SQLite), a storage engine is only as good as its crash consistency: when a host server experiences an abrupt power loss, kernel panic, or hardware reset, the database must restart gracefully without human intervention, detect uncommitted partial writes, and restore $100\%$ of acknowledged data.

You will assemble the individual subsystems (Write-Ahead Log, MemTable, SSTables, Bloom Filters, K-Way Compaction) into a unified, ergonomic public API providing `open`, `put`, `get`, `delete`, `flush`, and `compact`. You will implement a file manifest registry that assigns monotonically increasing sequence numbers to SSTables and WAL files, handle automated startup recovery by replaying the active WAL into memory on boot, and construct a **Chaos Fault-Injection Test Suite** that spawns child worker processes writing millions of keys, kills them ungracefully with `SIGKILL` (`kill -9`) mid-write, reopens the database, and programmatically asserts that every single acknowledged transaction is present, uncorrupted, and verified.

