## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Unified Engine Architecture & Component Coordination
All write and read operations are coordinated through the thread-safe `LsmDb` handle using `Arc<EngineInner>`:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                 LSM-DB UNIFIED ARCHITECTURE                             |
|                                                                                         |
|  Client Application Threads (Concurrent put / get / delete)                            |
|         |                                                                               |
|         v                                                                               |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | pub struct LsmDb { inner: Arc<EngineInner> }                                      |  |
|  |                                                                                   |  |
|  |  [ WalWriter ]        [ MemTableManager ]        [ SSTable Registry ]             |  |
|  |  - 000004.wal         - Active MemTable          - 000003.sst (Newest L0)         |  |
|  |  - sync_data()        - Frozen MemTable (flush)  - 000002.sst (Older L0)          |  |
|  |  - Appends mutations  - Capacity: 4MB            - 000001.sst (Compacted L1)      |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|         |                                                      |                        |
|         +---------- Background Flush & Compaction Worker <-----+                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Automated Boot & Crash Recovery Pipeline
When `LsmDb::open(dir_path)` executes:
1. **Directory Discovery**: Scans `dir_path` for all `.sst` files, extracts numerical IDs from filenames (e.g. `000003.sst`), and opens `SstableReader` instances sorted from newest to oldest.
2. **WAL Discovery**: Identifies the latest active `.wal` file (e.g. `000004.wal`).
3. **Replay Ingestion**: Replays all valid entries from the WAL into the initial Active MemTable. If the log concludes with a torn write from an ungraceful shutdown, recovery cleanly halts at the last valid CRC32 boundary without panicking.
4. **Database Ready**: Engine opens for client traffic with zero data loss for all acknowledged mutations.

### 3. Chaos Crash-Recovery Invariant
$$\forall K \in \text{Acknowledged Writes}, \quad \text{db.get}(K) = \text{Expected Value}$$
$$\text{Corrupted Bytes on Disk} = 0, \quad \text{Unrecoverable Errors on Boot} = 0$$

