# Phase 6 — High-Churn Autovacuum Tuning, Table Partitioning & End-to-End Load Benchmarks

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Master the production operations, storage optimization, and performance benchmarking of your PostgreSQL job queue. In PostgreSQL's Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) engine, updating a row (e.g. transitioning from `queued` $\to$ `running` $\to$ `done`) does not overwrite existing bytes in place; it writes a new tuple version and marks the previous version as a **dead tuple**. In a high-throughput queue processing millions of tasks daily, dead tuples accumulate at an enormous rate. If PostgreSQL's default background autovacuum daemon cannot keep up, **table bloat** occurs: disk space balloons, indexes become fragmented, and disk cache hit ratios collapse.

In this final phase, you will tune table-level storage parameters to handle heavy churn by reducing the autovacuum trigger scale factor from the default 20% down to 1% (`autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.01`) and increasing cost limits so autovacuum sweeps continuously in micro-batches without I/O throttling. You will implement an automated **Retention Pruner** that cleans up completed jobs older than a specified duration, and explore a **Declarative Range Partitioning** strategy where historical partitions can be instantly dropped in $0\text{ms}$ with zero WAL bloat. Finally, you will construct an end-to-end load test harness capable of producing and consuming 10,000 tasks across parallel workers, measuring sustained throughput ($>2,500$ jobs/sec) and validating healthy table statistics via `pg_stat_user_tables`.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. PostgreSQL MVCC Dead Tuple Generation & Table Bloat Lifecycle
Every task completion generates multiple dead tuples in storage:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         POSTGRESQL MVCC QUEUE CHURN LIFECYCLE                           |
|                                                                                         |
|  1. INSERT INTO job (state: 'queued')     -> Writes Tuple 1                             |
|  2. UPDATE job SET state = 'running'      -> Marks Tuple 1 DEAD, writes Tuple 2         |
|  3. UPDATE job SET state = 'done'         -> Marks Tuple 2 DEAD, writes Tuple 3         |
|                                                                                         |
|  NET RESULT FOR 1 COMPLETED TASK:                                                       |
|  - 1 Active Live Tuple (Tuple 3)                                                        |
|  - 2 DEAD Tuples (Tuple 1 & Tuple 2) taking up disk space in table pages!               |
|                                                                                         |
|  Default PostgreSQL Autovacuum Formula:                                                 |
|  Trigger Threshold = 50 + (0.20 * Table Rows).                                          |
|  -> On a table with 5,000,000 rows, vacuum only fires after 1,000,000 dead tuples!     |
|  -> The table swells with gigabytes of empty dead space before cleanup begins.          |
|                                                                                         |
|  Tuned Queue Autovacuum Formula:                                                        |
|  Trigger Threshold = 50 + (0.01 * Table Rows).                                          |
|  -> Vacuum fires after only 50,000 dead tuples!                                         |
|  -> Reclaims page slots continuously, maintaining a compact, stable disk footprint.     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Autovacuum Tuning Formula & Storage Parameters
$$\text{Dead Tuple Threshold} = \text{autovacuum\_vacuum\_threshold} + (\text{scale\_factor} \times \text{reltuples})$$
- Setting `autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.01` triggers cleanup when only $1\%$ of the table is dead.
- Setting `autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit = 2000` (default: 200) allows the vacuum worker to perform $10\times$ more page cleanups before pausing.
- Setting `autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay = 2` (default: 20ms) dramatically reduces sleep time between vacuum batches.

### 3. Declarative Range Partitioning vs Bulk `DELETE`
- Running `DELETE FROM job WHERE created_at < NOW() - interval '30 days'` requires sequential index scans, generates gigabytes of WAL logs (causing replication lag on read replicas), and leaves empty dead space requiring `VACUUM FULL` to reclaim.
- In contrast, partitioning by month (`PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at)`): dropping an old partition table (`DROP TABLE job_2026_01`) takes **$1\text{ millisecond}$**, generates zero dead tuples, and unlinks the OS file directly, immediately returning disk space to the host filesystem.

