# Phase 5 — Zero-Downtime Online Resharding, Dual-Writing & Checksum Verification

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Engineer an enterprise-grade **Online Resharding Engine** capable of scaling an active PostgreSQL cluster from 3 shards to 4 shards ($N=3 \to N=4$) live in production with zero downtime, zero data loss, and uninterrupted read/write availability. In a sharded database, the hardest operational challenge is cluster scale-out: when a 4th shard is added to the consistent hash ring, approximately $25\%$ of all existing tenants are assigned to the new shard. If you simply update the router immediately, all displaced tenants would experience complete data loss because their historical events still reside on older shards.

To migrate live tenants safely, you will implement a **5-Phase Zero-Downtime Resharding Protocol**:
1. **Shadow Shard Provisioning**: Boot `shard3` (port 5443) and apply identical DDL schemas.
2. **Live Dual-Writing**: Configure the router to mirror incoming writes for migrating tenants to both old and new shards concurrently.
3. **Resumable Chunked Bulk Backfill**: Copy historical tenant records in micro-batches (500 rows/batch) using `pgx.Batch` with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, logging checkpoints in a `resharding_progress` ledger so interrupted jobs resume automatically without restarting.
4. **Commutative XOR Checksum Parity Verification**: Mathematically certify $100\%$ data equality by computing order-independent bitwise XOR checksums over all row IDs and timestamps on both shards.
5. **Atomic Read/Write Cutover & Source Pruning**: Atomically flip the router's active ring to point exclusively to `shard3`, terminate dual-writing, and safely prune obsolete tenant records from source shards.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. The 5-Phase Zero-Downtime Resharding Pipeline
Follow the lifecycle of an online tenant migration during cluster expansion:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                     ZERO-DOWNTIME ONLINE RESHARDING LIFECYCLE                           |
|                                                                                         |
|  Phase 1: Dual-Write Active                                                             |
|  Application Write (Tenant 42)                                                          |
|        |                                                                                |
|        +-----> [Primary Write] --------> Source Shard (Shard 1)                         |
|        |                                                                                |
|        +-----> [Dual-Write Mirror] ----> Target Shard (Shard 3)                         |
|                                                                                         |
|  Phase 2: Historical Bulk Backfill (Background Worker)                                  |
|  Source Shard (1) =====[Batch Stream: 500 rows/batch]=====> Target Shard (3)            |
|  - Uses 'INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING' so live dual-writes take precedence!        |
|  - Records progress in 'resharding_progress' table.                                     |
|                                                                                         |
|  Phase 3: Cryptographic Parity Verification                                             |
|  Source XOR Checksum: 0x8F4E21BC  === Target XOR Checksum: 0x8F4E21BC (PARITY MATCH!)   |
|                                                                                         |
|  Phase 4: Read/Write Cutover (Atomic Ring Update)                                       |
|  - Router flips active Ring pointer to New Ring.                                        |
|  - Target Shard (3) is now the sole authoritative owner for Tenant 42!                  |
|                                                                                         |
|  Phase 5: Prune Obsolete Data                                                           |
|  - DELETE FROM events WHERE tenant_id = 42 on Source Shard (1).                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Dual-Write Conflict Resolution Mathematical Invariant
During Phase 2, a live write from an application can execute while the backfill worker is actively copying historical records:
$$\text{Live Write } W_t \text{ has timestamp } t > t_{\text{backfill}}$$
By structuring backfill inserts with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (tenant_id, id) DO NOTHING`, we guarantee that if a live write has already written an updated record to `shard3`, the backfill worker will **never overwrite** the newer row with an obsolete copy.

### 3. Commutative XOR Checksum Parity Math
Comparing database row counts is insufficient because row counts do not detect corrupted payloads or missing records. Computing an exact hash is challenging because PostgreSQL B-Trees do not return rows in identical order across shards. We formulate a **Commutative XOR Checksum**:
$$\text{Checksum}(T) = \bigoplus_{r \in \text{Rows}(T)} \text{CRC32}(r.\text{id} \,|\, r.\text{created\_at})$$
Because bitwise XOR ($\oplus$) is commutative ($A \oplus B = B \oplus A$) and associative ($A \oplus (B \oplus C) = (A \oplus B) \oplus C$), the resulting 32-bit checksum is identical regardless of the order in which rows are scanned on source vs target shards!

