# Phase 6 — Chaos Fault Injection, Circuit Breaking & Prometheus Latency Observability

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Equip your sharded PostgreSQL proxy with production-grade fault isolation, automated circuit breaking, real-time Prometheus telemetry, and validated chaos test suites. In a distributed sharded database, the foremost operational advantage over a monolithic architecture is **Blast Radius Containment**: when a physical database server suffers an unrecoverable hardware failure, kernel panic, or storage outage, only the subset of tenants mapped to that specific shard are impacted. The remainder of your cluster ($66\% - 75\%+$ of all active tenants) continues operating with $100\%$ normal uptime and single-digit millisecond latency.

However, achieving true blast radius containment requires resilient software engineering in the proxy layer:
1. Without circuit breakers, tenant queries targeting an offline shard block until low-level TCP connection timeouts (30+ seconds) expire. Under high request traffic, this thread starvation exhausts the router's connection pools and crashes the entire proxy, bringing down healthy shards as well.
2. Without high-cardinality observability, site reliability engineers cannot pinpoint which physical shard is failing or which tenant queries are causing latency spikes.

In this final phase, you will build an automated **Three-State Circuit Breaker** (Closed, Open, Half-Open) per shard that fast-fails dead shard queries in $<1\text{ ms}$, expose comprehensive Prometheus latency histograms (`p50`, `p95`, `p99`) via a dedicated `/metrics` endpoint, deploy a production HTTP service with graceful shutdown, and author an automated Chaos Fault Injection test suite verifying that healthy shards continue serving traffic while a crashed shard is isolated and automatically recovered.

