## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### 1. Shard Outage Blast Radius Containment
Observe how an outage on Shard 1 is strictly isolated, leaving Shard 0 and Shard 2 fully operational:

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                     SHARD OUTAGE BLAST RADIUS CONTAINMENT                               |
|                                                                                         |
|                   +-------------------------------+                                     |
|                   | Go Consistent-Hash Router     |                                     |
|                   +-------------------------------+                                     |
|                      /            |             \                                       |
|             Tenant 1/     Tenant 2|      Tenant 3\                                      |
|                    v              v               v                                     |
|              +----------+   +----------+    +----------+                                |
|              |  Shard 0 |   |  Shard 1 |    |  Shard 2 |                                |
|              | (Healthy)|   | (CRASHED)|    | (Healthy)|                                |
|              +----------+   +----------+    +----------+                                |
|                   |              |               |                                      |
|                   v              v               v                                      |
|               HTTP 200        HTTP 503        HTTP 200                                  |
|               (0.4 ms)      (Fast-Fail)       (0.4 ms)                                  |
|                                                                                         |
|  ISOLATION GUARANTEES:                                                                  |
|  - Tenant 1 & 3 experience 100% normal uptime and sub-millisecond response times!        |
|  - Tenant 2 fast-fails in < 1ms via Circuit Breaker rather than hanging for 30s!        |
|  - Cross-shard scatter queries return partial data flagged as 'degraded: true'!         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 2. Three-State Circuit Breaker Finite State Machine
Each physical shard pool is guarded by an autonomous state machine:

```
      [Successes]          +-----------------+        [Consecutive Failures >= 5]
  +----------------------->|     CLOSED      |----------------------------------+
  |                        | (Normal Flow)   |                                  |
  |                        +-----------------+                                  |
  |                                                                             v
  |  [Canary Probe Succeeded]                      [Timeout (5s) Expired]  +----------+
  +------------------------+-----------------------------------------------|   OPEN   |
                           |                                               | (Reject) |
                           v                                               +----------+
                   +---------------+                                            ^
                   |   HALF-OPEN   |--------------------------------------------+
                   | (Single Probe)|         [Canary Probe Failed]
                   +---------------+
```
- **Closed**: All queries pass through. Successes reset consecutive failure counters.
- **Open**: When 5 consecutive connection timeouts or database errors occur, the breaker trips to Open. All subsequent requests fail immediately with `ErrShardUnavailable` ($<1\text{ ms}$). Zero network calls are made to the dead shard.
- **Half-Open**: After a 5-second cooldown period, the breaker enters Half-Open and admits a single canary request. If the canary succeeds, the breaker resets to Closed; if it fails, it trips to Open for another 10 seconds.

### 3. Latency Histogram Bucket Formulation
To capture both microsecond query execution and slow queries without high-cardinality memory leaks, we configure bounded exponential histogram buckets:
$$\text{Buckets} = [0.0005, 0.001, 0.0025, 0.005, 0.01, 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5]$$
Labels are strictly restricted to low-cardinality tags: `{shard_id="shard0", op="read", status="success"}`. Never attach `tenant_id` to Prometheus metric labels!

